177
00:12:47,525 --> 00:12:48,966
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

178
00:12:48,986 --> 00:12:55,372
[SPEAKER_01]: Were these mostly feeling, or the outsets, I mean, or the assists, or both?

179
00:12:55,532 --> 00:12:56,152
[SPEAKER_00]: Both.

180
00:12:56,212 --> 00:12:56,813
[SPEAKER_00]: It was everything.

181
00:12:56,833 --> 00:13:00,176
[SPEAKER_00]: You had to start a double play or complete a double play.

182
00:13:05,928 --> 00:13:09,570
[SPEAKER_00]: Leo was a real good shortstop, a real good glove man.

183
00:13:09,670 --> 00:13:17,735
[SPEAKER_00]: He never did hit too much wherever he was, but even like when he was with the Yankees, they had such a great hitting team that they could afford to carry him.

184
00:13:17,775 --> 00:13:22,457
[SPEAKER_00]: But I don't know, he got in some trouble over there and they just let him go and Dan took him on.

185
00:13:22,477 --> 00:13:23,498
[SPEAKER_00]: Dan liked him very much.

186
00:13:24,330 --> 00:13:28,152
[SPEAKER_01]: Did you find a big adjustment problem from 3rd over to 2nd or did you play 2nd before?

187
00:13:28,252 --> 00:13:30,694
[SPEAKER_00]: No, well I had played 2nd base a great deal.

188
00:13:30,734 --> 00:13:32,835
[SPEAKER_00]: I played a little 3rd base.

189
00:13:32,855 --> 00:13:34,836
[SPEAKER_00]: 2nd base to me was more natural.

190
00:13:34,916 --> 00:13:35,977
[SPEAKER_00]: I had started there.

191
00:13:35,997 --> 00:13:39,379
[SPEAKER_00]: And then 3rd base was what they call the old man's home over there.

192
00:13:39,979 --> 00:13:44,402
[SPEAKER_00]: Just so you can knock down those line drives and have time to pick it up and throw the man out.

193
00:13:44,722 --> 00:13:45,983
[SPEAKER_00]: It wasn't no problem over there.

194
00:13:46,043 --> 00:13:46,924
[SPEAKER_01]: Pretty strong arm you had to have.

195
00:13:46,964 --> 00:13:48,885
[SPEAKER_00]: Well no, I didn't have too strong an arm.

196
00:13:48,925 --> 00:13:52,507
[SPEAKER_00]: I had hurt my arm the year before I went to Syracuse.

197
00:13:53,192 --> 00:14:01,480
[SPEAKER_00]: dislocated my elbow, but I got to throw the ball away quicker, like a lot like Robinson of the Baltimore Orioles.

198
00:14:01,560 --> 00:14:04,183
[SPEAKER_00]: I didn't have too strong an arm, but as soon as I got the ball, I let it go.

199
00:14:04,283 --> 00:14:07,105
[SPEAKER_00]: They knew it, and they'd get there in time to catch the ball.

200
00:14:07,386 --> 00:14:13,271
[SPEAKER_00]: So it made it a lot easier for me to play second base, because second base was more of a flip, not a long throw.

201
00:14:14,172 --> 00:14:17,035
[SPEAKER_01]: How about the men coming down and stealing?

202
00:14:18,345 --> 00:14:27,148
[SPEAKER_00]: Oh yeah, once in a while you would, but you learn to get away, how to protect yourself, especially on double plays when they're coming down.

203
00:14:27,608 --> 00:14:32,670
[SPEAKER_00]: The toughest part is when a fellow is stealing and a ground ball is in and you've got to make the double play.

204
00:14:32,730 --> 00:14:37,691
[SPEAKER_00]: Now you know you've got to get out of there, otherwise he's going to upset you, which I did.

205
00:14:37,831 --> 00:14:38,852
[SPEAKER_00]: They showed me how to do it.

206
00:14:39,272 --> 00:14:42,713
[SPEAKER_00]: I used to pivot on the inside and then make him get out of the way.

207
00:14:49,884 --> 00:14:52,065
[SPEAKER_01]: You had a very good year in 1931, too.

208
00:14:53,305 --> 00:14:56,127
[SPEAKER_00]: Yes, I believe it was 1931.

209
00:14:56,227 --> 00:14:57,647
[SPEAKER_00]: I know I hit over 300.

210
00:14:57,687 --> 00:14:59,408
[SPEAKER_01]: You played all 154 games.

211
00:14:59,468 --> 00:15:00,309
[SPEAKER_00]: I played all the games.

212
00:15:00,349 --> 00:15:07,872
[SPEAKER_00]: In fact, from when I first started in June, I believe I won about 400 or 500 games before I was out of a ball game.

213
00:15:09,653 --> 00:15:11,514
[SPEAKER_00]: You see, I was traded the next year to Brooklyn.

214
00:15:11,754 --> 00:15:12,514
[SPEAKER_01]: Why were you traded?

215
00:15:12,978 --> 00:15:15,379
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, Joe Strip and myself held out.

216
00:15:16,720 --> 00:15:23,122
[SPEAKER_00]: We wanted a little more money, and of course, Mr. Weil had lost a lot of money, and he said he couldn't afford it.

217
00:15:23,382 --> 00:15:26,864
[SPEAKER_00]: But after two years in the big leagues, I'd hit 312 and 315.

218
00:15:26,904 --> 00:15:30,705
[SPEAKER_00]: I thought I was entitled to more than what he was giving me at the time.

219
00:15:30,745 --> 00:15:31,866
[SPEAKER_00]: So we held out.

220
00:15:31,886 --> 00:15:42,490
[SPEAKER_00]: In fact, I was here in spring training in Tampa, Florida, when the deal was made with the Brooklyn Club, when Cincinnati got Babe Herman, Ernie Lombardi, and Wally Gilbert.

221
00:15:43,190 --> 00:15:49,352
[SPEAKER_00]: and they gave Brooklyn, Clyde Sueforth, Joe Strip, and myself.

222
00:15:49,432 --> 00:16:01,616
[SPEAKER_00]: At the time, Brooklyn Club was training at Clearwater, which is just about 18 miles across the bay here, and there was nothing for me to get over there, and Tampa, who was up in Orlando, those boys just rode up to Orlando and joined the club up there.

223
00:16:02,337 --> 00:16:03,737
[SPEAKER_01]: How did you feel about that trade?

224
00:16:04,317 --> 00:16:11,520
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, I liked it very much because I was living in Long Island City at the time, and Brooklyn is on the south shore of Long Island, see, and it was just a matter of

225
00:16:12,302 --> 00:16:14,543
[SPEAKER_00]: 15 or 20 miles from my house to the ballpark.

226
00:16:15,084 --> 00:16:20,067
[SPEAKER_00]: And I was there four years, and I saved a lot of money by my expenses, living away from home.

227
00:16:20,267 --> 00:16:22,648
[SPEAKER_01]: How about the ball club itself, playing with Brooklyn?

228
00:16:23,048 --> 00:16:23,889
[SPEAKER_00]: Oh, very good.

229
00:16:23,949 --> 00:16:25,530
[SPEAKER_00]: No, there was a good ball club at the time.

230
00:16:25,570 --> 00:16:29,512
[SPEAKER_00]: In fact, I still believe that we should have won the pennant that first year over there.

231
00:16:29,932 --> 00:16:31,033
[SPEAKER_00]: We had a good ball club.

232
00:16:31,493 --> 00:16:33,194
[SPEAKER_00]: We went under the last road trip.

233
00:16:34,515 --> 00:16:39,698
[SPEAKER_00]: It was about a 10-day road trip, leading by a game, and we came back off that road trip in third place.

234
00:16:40,320 --> 00:16:44,789
[SPEAKER_00]: We were beaten by the Cardinals, then the Cubs knocked us out of the first place.

235
00:16:45,290 --> 00:16:47,113
[SPEAKER_00]: And I believe the Cardinals went on to win.

236
00:16:48,676 --> 00:16:49,858
[SPEAKER_00]: It was a real good season.

237
00:16:49,898 --> 00:16:51,762
[SPEAKER_00]: We had some pretty good ballplayers over there then.

238
00:16:52,230 --> 00:16:52,870
[SPEAKER_01]: That'd be 32.

239
00:16:52,910 --> 00:16:55,311
[SPEAKER_01]: That'd be the Cubs, wouldn't it?

240
00:16:55,331 --> 00:16:57,412
[SPEAKER_00]: The Cubs or was it the Cardinals?

241
00:16:57,532 --> 00:16:59,312
[SPEAKER_00]: I know one of the two of them won it, yeah.

242
00:16:59,332 --> 00:17:00,093
[SPEAKER_01]: That's right, yeah.

243
00:17:00,193 --> 00:17:01,393
[SPEAKER_01]: Was Glenn Wright with you, King?

244
00:17:01,453 --> 00:17:02,714
[SPEAKER_00]: Yes, Glenn Wright was with us.

245
00:17:02,734 --> 00:17:04,734
[SPEAKER_00]: We had a real nice interview with him out in California.

246
00:17:04,754 --> 00:17:05,134
[SPEAKER_00]: Oh, wonderful.

247
00:17:05,174 --> 00:17:06,135
[SPEAKER_00]: Wonderful man, yeah.

248
00:17:06,175 --> 00:17:08,155
[SPEAKER_00]: I guess he was injured or something?

249
00:17:08,175 --> 00:17:09,036
[SPEAKER_00]: He hurt his arm.

250
00:17:09,056 --> 00:17:09,636
[SPEAKER_00]: He hurt his arm.

251
00:17:09,656 --> 00:17:12,457
[SPEAKER_00]: He couldn't make the long throw from shortstop.

252
00:17:13,277 --> 00:17:19,779
[SPEAKER_00]: We had Lonnie Fly and Mickey Finn, Gordon Slade.

253
00:17:20,219 --> 00:17:21,400
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, he was with us also.

254
00:17:23,657 --> 00:17:25,598
[SPEAKER_01]: How about the man Wilbur Robinson?

255
00:17:25,778 --> 00:17:26,018
[SPEAKER_00]: No.

256
00:17:26,799 --> 00:17:28,319
[SPEAKER_00]: Wilbur went out that year.

257
00:17:28,339 --> 00:17:28,840
[SPEAKER_00]: I went over there.

258
00:17:28,880 --> 00:17:31,021
[SPEAKER_00]: Max Carey took the job.

259
00:17:31,101 --> 00:17:32,581
[SPEAKER_00]: Max was only there one year.

260
00:17:33,622 --> 00:17:39,805
[SPEAKER_00]: Our coach, Casey Stengel, who was one of Max's coaches at the time, he took over the following year.

261
00:17:39,825 --> 00:17:41,946
[SPEAKER_00]: I was with Casey three years in Brooklyn.

262
00:17:42,126 --> 00:17:44,047
[SPEAKER_01]: How was Max Carey?

263
00:17:44,227 --> 00:17:45,768
[SPEAKER_00]: Max was a good manager.

264
00:17:47,131 --> 00:17:51,593
[SPEAKER_00]: He was a great believer in stealing bases and that sort of type of play.

265
00:17:51,613 --> 00:17:55,835
[SPEAKER_00]: The way he was, he was fast, real quick, good outfielder, good hitter, and run bases.

266
00:17:56,215 --> 00:17:58,816
[SPEAKER_00]: In fact, I couldn't do it because my legs weren't that good.

267
00:17:59,236 --> 00:18:03,978
[SPEAKER_00]: If my legs were real good, I don't believe I'd ever left the St. Louis Cardinals because they went in for speed.

268
00:18:03,998 --> 00:18:07,920
[SPEAKER_00]: That was Ricky's number one priority, speed, run, run, run, run.

269
00:18:07,960 --> 00:18:09,501
[SPEAKER_00]: And I couldn't do that.

270
00:18:09,561 --> 00:18:12,082
[SPEAKER_00]: I wish I could and it would have helped me a great deal, I know.

271
00:18:14,223 --> 00:18:15,263
[SPEAKER_01]: We're done here with Casey.

272
00:18:15,917 --> 00:18:17,920
[SPEAKER_00]: I was with Casey for three years, as I say.

273
00:18:17,940 --> 00:18:22,828
[SPEAKER_00]: I was with Max one year, Casey three years, and then I was traded to the Boston club.

274
00:18:22,848 --> 00:18:24,651
[SPEAKER_01]: How did you like Casey as a manager?

275
00:18:24,671 --> 00:18:25,572
[SPEAKER_00]: Casey was a good man.

276
00:18:25,612 --> 00:18:29,178
[SPEAKER_00]: I mean, he was better with the young fellas than he was with the old boys.

277
00:18:29,636 --> 00:18:31,497
[SPEAKER_00]: The older boy, he figured, knew how to play ball.

278
00:18:31,537 --> 00:18:35,981
[SPEAKER_00]: He'd leave him alone, but the young fellas, he would talk to us for hours, hours, hours, and hours.

279
00:18:36,061 --> 00:18:36,881
[SPEAKER_00]: Telling stories?

280
00:18:36,961 --> 00:18:37,962
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, not so much.

281
00:18:37,982 --> 00:18:42,726
[SPEAKER_00]: No, tell us the baseball things and things we should try to do and learn to do and all like that.

282
00:18:42,766 --> 00:18:43,606
[SPEAKER_00]: Did he understand?

283
00:18:44,027 --> 00:18:45,107
[SPEAKER_00]: Oh, yes.

284
00:18:45,568 --> 00:18:47,809
[SPEAKER_00]: He didn't talk that stingily at that time, no.

285
00:18:47,869 --> 00:18:49,871
[SPEAKER_00]: He was more of a baseball man.

286
00:18:50,672 --> 00:18:57,557
[SPEAKER_00]: And then, in fact, this funny thing, I was trained in Boston and McKechnie, Bill McKechnie was my manager up there for one year.

287
00:18:58,417 --> 00:18:58,978
[SPEAKER_01]: Thirty-six.

288
00:19:00,693 --> 00:19:03,157
[SPEAKER_00]: You know something we got Casey Stengel back up there.

289
00:19:03,497 --> 00:19:06,522
[SPEAKER_00]: I was back with him again for another four or five years, six years I believe.

290
00:19:07,624 --> 00:19:13,033
[SPEAKER_01]: He's in the Hall of Fame because he's a great manager, but his team didn't do so well those years.

291
00:19:14,029 --> 00:19:15,390
[SPEAKER_00]: He did well with the Yankees.

292
00:19:15,410 --> 00:19:19,833
[SPEAKER_00]: He had players that the Yankees could do what he wanted them to do.

293
00:19:21,274 --> 00:19:23,595
[SPEAKER_00]: He could say, go up and bunt, and they would bunt.

294
00:19:23,655 --> 00:19:27,078
[SPEAKER_00]: Or go ahead and hit the right field, and they would hit the right field.

295
00:19:27,178 --> 00:19:28,819
[SPEAKER_00]: Or he'd put you on your own.

296
00:19:29,219 --> 00:19:33,502
[SPEAKER_00]: Guys he had like Tomacho and Hendricks and Berra.

297
00:19:33,522 --> 00:19:35,343
[SPEAKER_00]: Those type of boys, he would just let them go.

298
00:19:35,363 --> 00:19:36,844
[SPEAKER_00]: He was on their own.

299
00:19:39,345 --> 00:19:43,588
[SPEAKER_01]: Were you with the Dodgers when Kerry made his remark about being still in the league?

300
00:19:43,928 --> 00:19:45,569
[SPEAKER_00]: Yes, that was 1934.

301
00:19:46,129 --> 00:19:47,330
[SPEAKER_00]: He was asked that question.

302
00:19:47,710 --> 00:19:50,151
[SPEAKER_00]: Someone asked him a question about, how about the Brooklyn Club?

303
00:19:50,171 --> 00:19:52,012
[SPEAKER_00]: And he said, oh, why are they still in the league?

304
00:19:52,553 --> 00:19:55,234
[SPEAKER_00]: But fortunately, that was 1934.

305
00:19:55,314 --> 00:19:57,516
[SPEAKER_00]: That's what it was.

306
00:19:57,536 --> 00:20:04,319
[SPEAKER_00]: Because it happened to be that the last two days of the season, Brooklyn was playing the Giants in the Polo Grounds.

307
00:20:04,800 --> 00:20:06,921
[SPEAKER_00]: And the Giants needed one game for a tie.

308
00:20:08,491 --> 00:20:15,115
[SPEAKER_00]: I believe the Cardinals were the team that year that won it, and we went in there and beat them two games and beat them out of the pennant.

309
00:20:15,856 --> 00:20:18,538
[SPEAKER_01]: Did that, his saying, really fire you fellas up?

310
00:20:18,558 --> 00:20:21,300
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, yeah, it did to a point that it made us feel bad, you know.

311
00:20:21,320 --> 00:20:27,804
[SPEAKER_00]: And then, of course, Casey had two or three meetings about that in particular, put it on our minds.

312
00:20:28,125 --> 00:20:32,388
[SPEAKER_00]: And we went in there, yeah, we went in there like we were the world champs at those two games.

313
00:20:32,708 --> 00:20:36,831
[SPEAKER_00]: We had finished, I believe, sixth or seventh in an eight-club league.

314
00:20:41,760 --> 00:20:46,724
[SPEAKER_01]: Your average fell off a little bit those years, but how'd you feel about that?

315
00:20:47,044 --> 00:20:50,147
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, the first year in Boston I hit 300.

316
00:20:50,187 --> 00:20:53,050
[SPEAKER_00]: I believe I hit 307, 308, something like that.

317
00:20:53,450 --> 00:20:55,512
[SPEAKER_00]: Then I went down, I went down a little bit up there.

318
00:20:56,172 --> 00:20:57,673
[SPEAKER_00]: Boston was a tough ballpark.

319
00:20:57,693 --> 00:21:05,540
[SPEAKER_00]: They had a big, big field and we played right alongside of the Charles River up there near Cambridge Pass, you know, right across from where Harvard is.

320
00:21:06,301 --> 00:21:07,642
[SPEAKER_00]: And the wind was always blowing in.

321
00:21:08,281 --> 00:21:11,143
[SPEAKER_00]: And I had to learn to hit the right field.

322
00:21:11,263 --> 00:21:12,885
[SPEAKER_00]: I was more of a full hitter at that time.

323
00:21:13,305 --> 00:21:19,910
[SPEAKER_00]: So after that, it started helping me again to spray my balls around, not try to go for the long ball or hit the ball hard anyway.

324
00:21:20,551 --> 00:21:23,794
[SPEAKER_00]: Just go for singles, which helped my average quite a bit after that.

325
00:21:24,494 --> 00:21:27,577
[SPEAKER_01]: How about Ebbets Field as a hitter, as a ballpark?

326
00:21:27,597 --> 00:21:29,238
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, no, Ebbets Field was good to hit at.

327
00:21:29,298 --> 00:21:37,985
[SPEAKER_00]: In fact, at that time, 1930, I got my first home run in Ebbets Field against Adolph Lukey, but the ball bounced into the stands.

328
00:21:38,481 --> 00:21:39,942
[SPEAKER_00]: See, that time was a home run.

329
00:21:40,263 --> 00:21:43,145
[SPEAKER_00]: And about two or three years later, they changed the ruling of that.

330
00:21:43,425 --> 00:21:45,007
[SPEAKER_00]: That had made it an automatic double.

331
00:21:45,407 --> 00:21:48,590
[SPEAKER_00]: You had to hit the ball into the stands in order to get the home run.

332
00:21:50,331 --> 00:21:51,792
[SPEAKER_00]: What was Mungo like?

333
00:21:52,573 --> 00:21:57,417
[SPEAKER_00]: Mungo was a real good, I wouldn't say a real good pitcher, but he could throw.

334
00:21:57,497 --> 00:21:58,358
[SPEAKER_00]: He could throw hard.

335
00:21:58,378 --> 00:21:59,339
[SPEAKER_00]: He could overpower you.

336
00:21:59,839 --> 00:22:04,503
[SPEAKER_00]: He developed a good curveball that made him a real, real, real tough pitcher.

337
00:22:04,961 --> 00:22:07,382
[SPEAKER_00]: But he wasn't the kind that could spot the ball.

338
00:22:07,882 --> 00:22:11,384
[SPEAKER_00]: He just overpowered it with his speed and good curve.

339
00:22:11,724 --> 00:22:12,305
[SPEAKER_01]: Control?

340
00:22:12,985 --> 00:22:14,346
[SPEAKER_00]: Yes, he had pretty good control.

341
00:22:15,246 --> 00:22:17,267
[SPEAKER_01]: He really had that.

342
00:22:17,287 --> 00:22:18,388
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, he had a big wind up.

343
00:22:18,408 --> 00:22:20,349
[SPEAKER_00]: He'd cock his leg way up in the air.

344
00:22:20,389 --> 00:22:24,871
[SPEAKER_00]: And look, by the time he brought it down, it looked like he was right on top of home plate.

345
00:22:24,891 --> 00:22:26,852
[SPEAKER_01]: The way pictures now have no wind in them.

346
00:22:27,473 --> 00:22:33,919
[SPEAKER_01]: You wonder back in the old days, people like Feller and Vance... We had Vance was on our club in Brooklyn that year.

347
00:22:34,279 --> 00:22:37,301
[SPEAKER_00]: And he's the one who used to wear that cut-up shirt, you know.

348
00:22:37,682 --> 00:22:40,964
[SPEAKER_00]: He used to cut his shirt up so when he went around, that would flare like that.

349
00:22:41,165 --> 00:22:42,946
[SPEAKER_00]: They finally stopped him from doing that, yeah.

350
00:22:44,347 --> 00:22:46,549
[SPEAKER_01]: Dazzy Vance, he was quite a picture.

351
00:22:46,769 --> 00:22:51,053
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, he was on his way out because Dazzy had come up to the big leagues late in his career.

352
00:22:52,024 --> 00:22:55,089
[SPEAKER_00]: And he still pitched, I believe, when he was 38 or 39 years old.

353
00:22:55,109 --> 00:22:56,230
[SPEAKER_01]: Why did he come up so late?

354
00:22:57,052 --> 00:22:57,833
[SPEAKER_00]: I really don't know.

355
00:22:57,853 --> 00:23:01,518
[SPEAKER_00]: I know they say that he come up around the age of 29, 30, somewhere around there.

356
00:23:01,678 --> 00:23:04,382
[SPEAKER_01]: When I first seen him in 1930, he could throw hard.

357
00:23:07,995 --> 00:23:14,339
[SPEAKER_00]: And in fact, before I started playing regularly again, I had started, and Dan took me out of the lineup.

358
00:23:14,979 --> 00:23:20,363
[SPEAKER_00]: One day, Dazzy was pitching against us, and they had us beat something like 7-2 or 7-1.

359
00:23:20,823 --> 00:23:23,384
[SPEAKER_00]: And Howley said, how would you like to hit against Vance?

360
00:23:23,745 --> 00:23:27,607
[SPEAKER_00]: I said, well, sure, I'd like to hit against him, just to see how hard he throws.

361
00:23:27,987 --> 00:23:28,287
[SPEAKER_00]: He did.

362
00:23:28,307 --> 00:23:30,729
[SPEAKER_00]: He put me in the pinch hit in the eighth inning of the ballgame.

363
00:23:31,089 --> 00:23:32,750
[SPEAKER_00]: And I'll say one thing, he could throw hard.

364
00:23:32,890 --> 00:23:33,951
[SPEAKER_00]: What did he do?

365
00:23:34,531 --> 00:23:36,153
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, I don't exactly remember.

366
00:23:36,173 --> 00:23:39,216
[SPEAKER_01]: No, I don't.

367
00:23:42,499 --> 00:23:44,961
[SPEAKER_01]: How about another officer?

368
00:23:44,981 --> 00:23:47,023
[SPEAKER_01]: Who was catching with the Dodgers?

369
00:23:47,603 --> 00:23:48,084
[SPEAKER_00]: Lopez.

370
00:23:50,292 --> 00:23:58,797
[SPEAKER_00]: Hank Deberry, who used to catch Vance, and then he would catch one or two of the other pitchers, and Lopez would catch the majority of the pitchers.

371
00:23:58,837 --> 00:24:07,102
[SPEAKER_00]: But then, I would say around May or June of that 1930, Al was put in and he was the regular catcher from there on.

372
00:24:07,642 --> 00:24:09,883
[SPEAKER_01]: That was his first year then?

373
00:24:09,923 --> 00:24:11,144
[SPEAKER_01]: His first full year, yes.

374
00:24:11,704 --> 00:24:11,984
[SPEAKER_00]: 1930.

375
00:24:12,044 --> 00:24:14,606
[SPEAKER_00]: Then he stayed there 1930.

376
00:24:15,326 --> 00:24:16,207
[SPEAKER_00]: He left with me 1935.

377
00:24:18,678 --> 00:24:20,459
[SPEAKER_00]: the fall of 35 to go to Boston.

378
00:24:21,300 --> 00:24:22,301
[SPEAKER_00]: We went up there together.

379
00:24:25,784 --> 00:24:27,585
[SPEAKER_00]: Oh, he was a good receiver.

380
00:24:27,945 --> 00:24:35,892
[SPEAKER_00]: In fact, the first two years he hit real, real good, but he caught, I believe the first year he caught something like 145 games out of, and it just tired him out.

381
00:24:35,912 --> 00:24:38,214
[SPEAKER_00]: He just, he got so that he couldn't hardly swing the bat.

382
00:24:38,634 --> 00:24:45,900
[SPEAKER_00]: He had a 300 year the first year he played, but then from there on he went down, oh, around 260, 65, somewhere there, but he caught

383
00:24:46,478 --> 00:24:47,599
[SPEAKER_00]: He caught for a long time.

384
00:24:47,619 --> 00:24:49,861
[SPEAKER_00]: In fact, he caught 18 years in the big leagues.

385
00:24:50,681 --> 00:24:53,324
[SPEAKER_01]: I wanted to talk to him, but I couldn't find his name in the phone book.

386
00:24:53,384 --> 00:24:54,244
[SPEAKER_01]: I guess he's not listed.

387
00:24:54,585 --> 00:24:55,706
[SPEAKER_00]: No, I don't believe he is.

388
00:24:55,726 --> 00:24:55,926
[SPEAKER_00]: No.

389
00:24:57,087 --> 00:24:59,549
[SPEAKER_01]: Who was your Cincinnati catcher again?

390
00:24:59,969 --> 00:25:07,175
[SPEAKER_00]: At that time, we had Val Pacinic, Johnny Gooch, and a young boy by the name of Welder.

391
00:25:07,602 --> 00:25:19,172
[SPEAKER_00]: Clyde Tukvoth was with us and another young kid, we had ball catchers there for quite a while, Asby Johnson, they finally shipped him out to the minor leagues.

392
00:25:19,192 --> 00:25:22,475
[SPEAKER_00]: He won't use the name of Asby, but his name was Asby Johnson.

393
00:25:22,495 --> 00:25:23,676
[SPEAKER_01]: How about batting against Carl Hubble?

394
00:25:35,052 --> 00:25:37,674
[SPEAKER_00]: Not that I want to brag or anything, but I could hit Carl good.

395
00:25:37,894 --> 00:25:44,340
[SPEAKER_00]: At that particular time, I was strictly a low-ball hitter, and Carl was a low-ball pitcher with his screwball.

396
00:25:44,360 --> 00:25:45,961
[SPEAKER_00]: He'd pitch it in and out, in and out.

397
00:25:46,742 --> 00:25:49,524
[SPEAKER_00]: Luckily, I would say, I bet I hit 300 over him.

398
00:25:50,084 --> 00:25:57,270
[SPEAKER_00]: There were some guys in the league that weren't as good a pitcher as him and used to get me out more than Carl did.

399
00:25:57,971 --> 00:26:00,393
[SPEAKER_00]: But I had good success against Hubbell.

400
00:26:00,713 --> 00:26:03,255
[SPEAKER_00]: The fellow at New York in particular gave me trouble.

401
00:26:03,843 --> 00:26:05,163
[SPEAKER_00]: was Leroy Pommaly.

402
00:26:06,064 --> 00:26:16,828
[SPEAKER_00]: Oh, gee, Leroy, he could throw the ball hard, but that's the thing, you were a little leery of getting sculled, you know, and at that time, in that particular time, we never wore helmets at all.

403
00:26:17,588 --> 00:26:18,108
[SPEAKER_01]: No helmets.

404
00:26:18,528 --> 00:26:26,531
[SPEAKER_01]: He says in his career he hit five batters in the head, minor in the majors, and he was no good after that.

405
00:26:26,691 --> 00:26:30,273
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, it got to bother him a lot, I know.

406
00:26:30,353 --> 00:26:31,513
[SPEAKER_00]: One year in the big leagues he hit

407
00:26:32,387 --> 00:26:36,308
[SPEAKER_00]: three fellas in the arm and broke two or three of their wrists.

408
00:26:36,328 --> 00:26:38,269
[SPEAKER_00]: He didn't mean to do it, just that wild.

409
00:26:38,729 --> 00:26:41,249
[SPEAKER_00]: He tried to throw strikes, but the ball would get away from him.

410
00:26:41,810 --> 00:26:44,030
[SPEAKER_01]: He's pretty well crippled up now.

411
00:26:44,410 --> 00:26:52,012
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, well, I imagine after so many years of baseball and injuries that you get, my knees bother me a little bit.

412
00:26:52,593 --> 00:26:56,254
[SPEAKER_00]: I've been hit there a couple of times on these, around second base.

413
00:26:56,634 --> 00:26:58,114
[SPEAKER_01]: Breaking up a double play.

414
00:26:58,214 --> 00:26:58,334
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

415
00:27:04,198 --> 00:27:07,119
[SPEAKER_01]: Schumacher was real good.

416
00:27:07,480 --> 00:27:09,280
[SPEAKER_00]: He was an overhand sinker ball pitcher.

417
00:27:09,660 --> 00:27:16,163
[SPEAKER_00]: He came up in 1930, I believe from St. Louis College up in New York State somewhere.

418
00:27:16,183 --> 00:27:18,763
[SPEAKER_00]: A real good competitor, a good hitter.

419
00:27:18,984 --> 00:27:21,724
[SPEAKER_00]: So was Freddie Fitzsimmons on the ball club at that time.

420
00:27:22,145 --> 00:27:23,705
[SPEAKER_00]: They were three great pitchers.

421
00:27:23,945 --> 00:27:25,266
[SPEAKER_01]: He just died last night.

422
00:27:25,286 --> 00:27:26,146
[SPEAKER_01]: Yes, I know he did.

423
00:27:26,166 --> 00:27:27,366
[SPEAKER_00]: I read that in the sporting news.

424
00:27:27,687 --> 00:27:30,768
[SPEAKER_01]: We were out there in California two years ago and I got to talk to him.

425
00:27:32,322 --> 00:27:35,263
[SPEAKER_01]: Who was your, what was your line-up up there in Boston?

426
00:27:35,543 --> 00:27:36,763
[SPEAKER_01]: Remember when you went up there?

427
00:27:37,023 --> 00:27:54,346
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, I don't remember the particular line-up, but I can, well, see, Lopez caught, we had Baxter Jordan at first base, I played second base, we had Billy Urbanski, and then after he, Eddie Miller, remember, later went to Cincinnati, Eddie was with us, and Pinky Whitney at third base.

428
00:27:54,446 --> 00:28:01,387
[SPEAKER_00]: Lethfield, Adel, Howley, Wally Berger, and Gene... Moore.

429
00:28:01,904 --> 00:28:03,766
[SPEAKER_00]: Gene Moore was our right fielder.

430
00:28:04,586 --> 00:28:08,530
[SPEAKER_00]: Pitches, we had Danny McFadden, Fetty, Turner.

431
00:28:08,950 --> 00:28:10,151
[SPEAKER_01]: Oh yeah, that was the year they broke in.

432
00:28:10,171 --> 00:28:13,534
[SPEAKER_00]: They broke in, they won both 20 games, I believe, yes.

433
00:28:14,195 --> 00:28:15,956
[SPEAKER_01]: They were really late.

434
00:28:16,056 --> 00:28:17,417
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, they were, yes.

435
00:28:17,938 --> 00:28:21,741
[SPEAKER_00]: Johnny, I don't believe John, come up until 31 or 32.

436
00:28:22,141 --> 00:28:24,884
[SPEAKER_00]: But then he pitched until he was almost 40.

437
00:28:25,724 --> 00:28:27,386
[SPEAKER_00]: Then later on he became a relief pitcher.

438
00:28:31,288 --> 00:28:32,509
[SPEAKER_01]: That was an interesting team.

439
00:28:33,329 --> 00:28:36,151
[SPEAKER_00]: Danny McFadden was one of our great pitchers up there, too, that year.

440
00:28:36,191 --> 00:28:39,413
[SPEAKER_00]: He came from the Red Sox to the Yankees to the Boston Braves.

441
00:28:39,793 --> 00:28:41,574
[SPEAKER_01]: He wore glasses, didn't he?

442
00:28:41,694 --> 00:28:42,675
[SPEAKER_01]: Yes, yes, real thick ones.

443
00:28:42,715 --> 00:28:43,875
[SPEAKER_01]: He was one of the first ones to do that.

444
00:28:43,895 --> 00:28:44,676
[SPEAKER_01]: There were a couple of them.

445
00:28:44,696 --> 00:28:48,918
[SPEAKER_00]: Yes, there was, like, George Teposa, the infielder with the Cardinals, wore glasses.

446
00:28:49,378 --> 00:28:53,481
[SPEAKER_00]: I believe one of the Hill boys, Common Hill, wore glasses.

447
00:28:53,581 --> 00:28:59,064
[SPEAKER_01]: In fact, he said he missed the 1927 season when the Pirates won the pennant.

448
00:28:59,721 --> 00:29:11,375
[SPEAKER_01]: He won 22 games, and he says he could have won 23 because he's pitching the last game was ahead by a couple of runs with two out in the fifth inning, just when he would get credit for the game if he got one more man out.

449
00:29:11,475 --> 00:29:16,400
[SPEAKER_01]: He made a violent pitch and knocked his glasses off and stepped off.

450
00:29:16,440 --> 00:29:19,103
[SPEAKER_01]: He didn't get the victory.

451
00:29:19,484 --> 00:29:20,365
[SPEAKER_01]: He laughed about that.

452
00:29:21,429 --> 00:29:22,490
[SPEAKER_01]: I wonder how it must have been.

453
00:29:22,550 --> 00:29:24,052
[SPEAKER_01]: Of course, they do it quite often now.

454
00:29:24,452 --> 00:29:30,238
[SPEAKER_00]: We even got catchers now wearing glasses, which was a rarity.

455
00:29:30,578 --> 00:29:32,440
[SPEAKER_00]: Ever see a catcher with glasses back then?

456
00:29:32,460 --> 00:29:35,142
[SPEAKER_00]: You'd think, you know, afraid you're going to break them and all like that.

457
00:29:35,162 --> 00:29:36,243
[SPEAKER_00]: Did you ever get hit in the head badly?

458
00:29:36,583 --> 00:29:38,345
[SPEAKER_00]: Yes, a couple of times I did.

459
00:29:38,405 --> 00:29:39,846
[SPEAKER_00]: Were you serious?

460
00:29:39,866 --> 00:29:42,309
[SPEAKER_00]: No, it wasn't serious.

461
00:29:42,409 --> 00:29:43,250
[SPEAKER_00]: One hurt me a little bit.

462
00:29:43,290 --> 00:29:44,371
[SPEAKER_00]: It hit me in the back of the neck.

463
00:29:45,411 --> 00:29:47,892
[SPEAKER_00]: It's the thing of losing the ball, that's the thing.

464
00:29:48,312 --> 00:29:59,674
[SPEAKER_00]: Especially in the Cubs ballpark, before they changed it, they used to have a low center field and all the people in the summertime with the white shirts would sit out there and the ball was thrown out of the way and you couldn't pick it up.

465
00:30:00,054 --> 00:30:02,794
[SPEAKER_00]: And they had some hard throwers there.

466
00:30:03,134 --> 00:30:07,515
[SPEAKER_00]: Twice I could hit there and once in Cincinnati.

467
00:30:22,892 --> 00:30:31,299
[SPEAKER_00]: They used to have a low center field, and all the people in the summertime with the white shirts would sit out there, and the ball was thrown out of the way, and you couldn't pick it up.

468
00:30:31,679 --> 00:30:33,100
[SPEAKER_00]: And they had some hard throwers there.

469
00:30:34,781 --> 00:30:39,165
[SPEAKER_00]: Twice I got hit there, and once in Cincinnati.

470
00:30:40,626 --> 00:30:41,847
[SPEAKER_01]: Glancing blows?

471
00:30:42,027 --> 00:30:44,769
[SPEAKER_00]: Yes, one was a glancing blow over the top of the head.

472
00:30:45,410 --> 00:30:51,695
[SPEAKER_00]: The one that hurt me a little bit was one right back of the neck, that I did finally have a little calcification up there

473
00:30:52,669 --> 00:30:56,191
[SPEAKER_00]: They were going to take out and finally said, it didn't bother me, just let it alone.

474
00:30:56,711 --> 00:30:59,573
[SPEAKER_00]: It took me about a week before I was able to start playing again.

475
00:30:59,773 --> 00:31:01,093
[SPEAKER_01]: None of them knocked you out though?

476
00:31:01,214 --> 00:31:03,095
[SPEAKER_00]: No, momentarily, that's about all, yeah.

477
00:31:03,115 --> 00:31:03,695
[SPEAKER_01]: Stay in the game?

478
00:31:03,975 --> 00:31:05,736
[SPEAKER_00]: No, the second one, no, I didn't.

479
00:31:06,296 --> 00:31:06,716
[SPEAKER_00]: I got out.

480
00:31:06,876 --> 00:31:07,837
[SPEAKER_00]: The first one I stayed.

481
00:31:09,418 --> 00:31:12,199
[SPEAKER_01]: If they'd had helmets then, would you have thought they were a good idea?

482
00:31:12,499 --> 00:31:12,940
[SPEAKER_00]: Oh, yes.

483
00:31:13,940 --> 00:31:14,821
[SPEAKER_00]: I think it was a good idea.

484
00:31:14,841 --> 00:31:16,281
[SPEAKER_00]: I think maybe it would have helped a bunch of us.

485
00:31:16,381 --> 00:31:17,422
[SPEAKER_00]: I remember

486
00:31:18,326 --> 00:31:23,949
[SPEAKER_00]: Bucky Wallace hitting Billy Jurgis in the Polo Grounds just above the ear.

487
00:31:24,289 --> 00:31:25,650
[SPEAKER_00]: Now the helmet would have helped him.

488
00:31:26,210 --> 00:31:32,754
[SPEAKER_00]: I remember Mungo hitting a boy by the name of Marshall with the New York Giants in the head.

489
00:31:33,034 --> 00:31:37,056
[SPEAKER_00]: He wasn't thrown at him, but just the ball got away from him and hit him, and that was the end of that poor boy's career.

490
00:31:37,997 --> 00:31:39,557
[SPEAKER_00]: And there were several incidents.

491
00:31:39,638 --> 00:31:43,640
[SPEAKER_00]: Now maybe that boy in Cleveland that was killed, remember Chapman?

492
00:31:44,404 --> 00:31:47,845
[SPEAKER_00]: Maybe he might have been saved, of course, if they had helmets at that time.

493
00:31:47,925 --> 00:31:48,686
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

494
00:31:49,866 --> 00:31:54,608
[SPEAKER_01]: But you hear some of the old-timers kind of look down their noses at helmets.

495
00:31:54,908 --> 00:31:57,229
[SPEAKER_01]: It's something almost unmanly.

496
00:31:57,309 --> 00:31:57,729
[SPEAKER_01]: Oh, no.

497
00:31:58,309 --> 00:32:04,471
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, I mean, I guess they're thinking maybe they shouldn't be wearing them, but I'm glad to see the boys wearing them.

498
00:32:04,611 --> 00:32:07,252
[SPEAKER_00]: I see a lot of times boys getting hit.

499
00:32:07,612 --> 00:32:09,433
[SPEAKER_00]: Glancing blows up, which helps a lot.

500
00:32:09,693 --> 00:32:12,034
[SPEAKER_00]: It stops concussions and things of that sort.

501
00:32:12,610 --> 00:32:14,951
[SPEAKER_01]: Were you a little gun-shy if you got back there?

502
00:32:14,991 --> 00:32:16,172
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, for a game or two.

503
00:32:16,192 --> 00:32:18,934
[SPEAKER_00]: But they take you out and give you batting practice and help you.

504
00:32:19,294 --> 00:32:27,979
[SPEAKER_00]: And I know before I went back, I can't remember who was the manager at the time, but he said, I want you to go up there and take batting practice without knowing what's being pitched.

505
00:32:28,559 --> 00:32:30,220
[SPEAKER_00]: They get me back in stride of hitting.

506
00:32:30,500 --> 00:32:32,501
[SPEAKER_00]: And that helps you going back into the game, see?

507
00:32:32,822 --> 00:32:33,962
[SPEAKER_01]: Got to go right back in there.

508
00:32:34,002 --> 00:32:35,303
[SPEAKER_00]: That's right, yeah.

509
00:32:35,343 --> 00:32:36,484
[SPEAKER_00]: You've got to make up your mind.

510
00:32:36,504 --> 00:32:37,044
[SPEAKER_00]: You've got to do it.

511
00:32:37,084 --> 00:32:40,446
[SPEAKER_00]: And once you get one game under your belt, then you're all right again, yeah.

512
00:32:41,246 --> 00:32:42,207
[SPEAKER_01]: How about the old days?

513
00:32:43,322 --> 00:32:44,563
[SPEAKER_00]: He was no easier boy to hit.

514
00:32:44,623 --> 00:32:49,065
[SPEAKER_00]: Now this is a boy that good fastball used to bear into a right-hander.

515
00:32:49,385 --> 00:32:53,587
[SPEAKER_00]: He's like what they said, he slung the ball, as he used to say.

516
00:32:53,907 --> 00:32:57,529
[SPEAKER_00]: He was a slingshot artist, you know, and the ball would bear into a right-hand hitter.

517
00:32:57,729 --> 00:33:02,452
[SPEAKER_00]: A lot of times he started in a little too closely and you better get out of the way, but he was rapid.

518
00:33:02,592 --> 00:33:03,712
[SPEAKER_00]: He was really, really fast.

519
00:33:03,992 --> 00:33:05,313
[SPEAKER_00]: A lot faster than his brother Paul.

520
00:33:05,904 --> 00:33:07,205
[SPEAKER_01]: Did he talk during the game?

521
00:33:07,305 --> 00:33:09,106
[SPEAKER_00]: Oh, sure.

522
00:33:09,166 --> 00:33:10,788
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, he'd lob or do anything.

523
00:33:10,828 --> 00:33:15,851
[SPEAKER_00]: In fact, one day in Brooklyn, he came in our clubhouse and conducted Casey Stengel's meeting.

524
00:33:16,271 --> 00:33:19,334
[SPEAKER_00]: Told him exactly how he was going to pitch to us, and pitched a three-hitter.

525
00:33:20,114 --> 00:33:21,415
[SPEAKER_01]: How'd the players react to that?

526
00:33:21,775 --> 00:33:22,776
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, they didn't mind.

527
00:33:22,796 --> 00:33:24,858
[SPEAKER_00]: He was the comical sort of a guy.

528
00:33:24,878 --> 00:33:25,658
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, a lot of fun.

529
00:33:26,519 --> 00:33:28,120
[SPEAKER_00]: O'Doul used to get a big kick out of him.

530
00:33:28,180 --> 00:33:29,681
[SPEAKER_00]: O'Doul used to con him a little bit.

531
00:33:29,721 --> 00:33:33,904
[SPEAKER_00]: And he always said, gee, that lefty's a good guy, but lefty'd always come up with a couple of hits off him.

532
00:33:33,964 --> 00:33:34,284
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

533
00:33:35,790 --> 00:33:37,351
[SPEAKER_01]: There's another character, Lucky O'Doul.

534
00:33:37,371 --> 00:33:39,032
[SPEAKER_01]: Do you have any O'Doul stories?

535
00:33:39,832 --> 00:33:48,537
[SPEAKER_00]: No, well, all I know is that we played with this in Brooklyn, and he always wore that green suit, that Irish suit, and Lucky was a real good hitter.

536
00:33:49,118 --> 00:33:50,178
[SPEAKER_00]: He had hurt his arm.

537
00:33:50,478 --> 00:33:54,741
[SPEAKER_00]: He was a pitcher before going to the outfield, and hurt his arm, and he couldn't throw very well.

538
00:33:55,862 --> 00:33:56,642
[SPEAKER_00]: And he had good legs.

539
00:33:56,662 --> 00:34:03,326
[SPEAKER_00]: He could get to a ball, he'd hold on to it, but when it was too deep, somebody had to go out to second base for the first base, and had to go out and help him throw that ball in.

540
00:34:04,997 --> 00:34:11,260
[SPEAKER_01]: Somebody said that Old Duo was in a town in about ten minutes and he could have run for mayor or something like that.

541
00:34:11,320 --> 00:34:14,522
[SPEAKER_00]: He's a very comical man and a very, very nice man.

542
00:34:16,022 --> 00:34:20,665
[SPEAKER_01]: This thing here doesn't tell you when it's over, so I've got to keep my eye on it.

543
00:34:26,857 --> 00:34:32,161
[SPEAKER_01]: How about the umpires?

544
00:34:32,201 --> 00:34:36,304
[SPEAKER_00]: Bill was one of the best umpires we had in the major leagues at that time.

545
00:34:36,684 --> 00:34:45,370
[SPEAKER_00]: Of course, there are real good young umpires now or even later on after Bill, but we did have a bunch of older type like Moran.

546
00:34:45,390 --> 00:34:49,393
[SPEAKER_00]: Remember Charlie Moran from Center College in Kentucky?

547
00:34:50,057 --> 00:34:56,141
[SPEAKER_00]: Charlie was a good man, Bill Crammond, Cy Riggler was another big tall man.

548
00:34:56,421 --> 00:35:00,363
[SPEAKER_00]: Of course, they had a lot of the old timers and they were just getting through when I was breaking in.

549
00:35:00,863 --> 00:35:03,625
[SPEAKER_00]: Then the younger boys started coming in like Larry Getz.

550
00:35:03,725 --> 00:35:05,246
[SPEAKER_00]: Was Ensley still around?

551
00:35:05,266 --> 00:35:05,526
[SPEAKER_00]: Who?

552
00:35:05,766 --> 00:35:06,467
[SPEAKER_01]: Bill Ensley.

553
00:35:06,867 --> 00:35:08,568
[SPEAKER_00]: No, he was in the American League at the time.

554
00:35:08,588 --> 00:35:10,149
[SPEAKER_00]: See, I started in the National League.

555
00:35:10,209 --> 00:35:14,552
[SPEAKER_00]: No, I can't think of exactly who.

556
00:35:14,592 --> 00:35:16,953
[SPEAKER_00]: I know that Bay Pinelli was around.

557
00:35:18,830 --> 00:35:21,210
[SPEAKER_00]: Yes, Ernie Quigley was still in baseball.

558
00:35:21,511 --> 00:35:25,352
[SPEAKER_00]: And then he retired, I believe, and went back to Kansas to teach.

559
00:35:25,392 --> 00:35:26,973
[SPEAKER_00]: He was a teacher of some sort, yeah.

560
00:35:26,993 --> 00:35:30,154
[SPEAKER_01]: Did you get thrown out of any games?

561
00:35:31,354 --> 00:35:32,195
[SPEAKER_00]: Not too many.

562
00:35:32,275 --> 00:35:35,056
[SPEAKER_00]: I got thrown out of one game, not being my fault.

563
00:35:35,096 --> 00:35:37,677
[SPEAKER_00]: Now there's another man I just thought about.

564
00:35:37,697 --> 00:35:40,458
[SPEAKER_00]: The man that threw me out of the game, Beans Rarity.

565
00:35:42,234 --> 00:35:43,455
[SPEAKER_00]: We had a little problem with beans.

566
00:35:43,475 --> 00:35:46,297
[SPEAKER_00]: The club had a little problem with beans at St. Louis one day.

567
00:35:46,938 --> 00:35:50,540
[SPEAKER_00]: And I had left the dugout to go in the back.

568
00:35:51,221 --> 00:35:54,163
[SPEAKER_00]: And when I come back up a hit or two later, I went up to the plate.

569
00:35:54,183 --> 00:35:58,246
[SPEAKER_00]: And I questioned one of his pitches that he called a strike on me.

570
00:35:58,266 --> 00:36:00,989
[SPEAKER_00]: And right without saying another word, he said I was out of the ballgame.

571
00:36:01,029 --> 00:36:01,709
[SPEAKER_00]: And I said, why?

572
00:36:01,989 --> 00:36:03,390
[SPEAKER_00]: I just asked you about the pitch.

573
00:36:03,430 --> 00:36:06,052
[SPEAKER_00]: He said, well, that was the reason he had told everybody on the bench.

574
00:36:06,313 --> 00:36:08,614
[SPEAKER_00]: The next one squawks about any kind of pitches out of the ballgame.

575
00:36:08,895 --> 00:36:09,435
[SPEAKER_00]: And I was out.

576
00:36:09,535 --> 00:36:10,596
[SPEAKER_00]: I didn't know anything about it.

577
00:36:11,307 --> 00:36:20,977
[SPEAKER_00]: But then he apologized to me later, which I told him that I wasn't on the bench when he come over to let everybody know what's going to happen, and I was the guilty one.

578
00:36:22,196 --> 00:36:26,197
[SPEAKER_01]: Were you thrown out of any others for a better reason?

579
00:36:26,597 --> 00:36:26,897
[SPEAKER_00]: No.

580
00:36:27,217 --> 00:36:29,778
[SPEAKER_00]: I don't even remember being thrown out of any more games, no.

581
00:36:29,818 --> 00:36:31,138
[SPEAKER_00]: Did you argue much?

582
00:36:31,198 --> 00:36:31,698
[SPEAKER_00]: No.

583
00:36:32,258 --> 00:36:32,719
[SPEAKER_00]: They knew.

584
00:36:33,859 --> 00:36:38,300
[SPEAKER_00]: I didn't squawk too much, but if I had a squawk come, they knew something was radically wrong.

585
00:36:38,800 --> 00:36:39,700
[SPEAKER_00]: And they were good to me.

586
00:36:39,740 --> 00:36:46,042
[SPEAKER_00]: Now, they'd say later on, like if I tagged the man, they thought I didn't tag him, they'd come back and say, did you really tag the man?

587
00:36:46,062 --> 00:36:47,302
[SPEAKER_00]: And I'd say, yes, I did.

588
00:36:47,342 --> 00:36:49,082
[SPEAKER_00]: I tagged him on the side or something like that.

589
00:36:49,162 --> 00:36:50,083
[SPEAKER_00]: And they would believe you.

590
00:36:50,583 --> 00:36:51,723
[SPEAKER_00]: And they were real, real good.

591
00:36:51,983 --> 00:37:03,126
[SPEAKER_00]: I know later on I went to coaching, coaching third base, and two or three times I've seen fellows make swipes at my men and hit them, tag them, and the umpires would call them safe.

592
00:37:03,787 --> 00:37:12,329
[SPEAKER_00]: And then they weren't in position at the time to see the tag, but two or three years later they'd come over and ask you, was he tagged or not?

593
00:37:12,629 --> 00:37:14,010
[SPEAKER_00]: I wouldn't lie to them, tell them the truth.

594
00:37:14,790 --> 00:37:17,351
[SPEAKER_00]: Yes, he was tagged, and I think they liked me for it.

595
00:37:19,816 --> 00:37:23,401
[SPEAKER_01]: Do you think they do a pretty good job overall?

596
00:37:24,222 --> 00:37:27,667
[SPEAKER_00]: They do a lot better job now because they only cover one base.

597
00:37:27,747 --> 00:37:28,828
[SPEAKER_00]: Now they got four umpires.

598
00:37:29,349 --> 00:37:33,455
[SPEAKER_00]: Years ago we had only two for a long time and that made it a little tough.

599
00:37:33,855 --> 00:37:37,100
[SPEAKER_00]: Now the umpire at home plate had to coil the balls down both lines.

600
00:37:37,440 --> 00:37:39,540
[SPEAKER_00]: fair or foul, which is a tough thing.

601
00:37:39,560 --> 00:37:48,622
[SPEAKER_00]: You take curving balls 340, 50 feet down the line, it's tough to sell it, whether it hit the line, off the line, fair or foul, which is real, real bad.

602
00:37:48,642 --> 00:37:53,723
[SPEAKER_00]: But now, as they say, World Series, they have six, two down each line, makes it a lot easier.

603
00:37:53,983 --> 00:37:54,743
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, that's right.

604
00:37:55,223 --> 00:37:57,963
[SPEAKER_00]: And I think it's a lot nicer, a lot better.

605
00:37:58,644 --> 00:38:00,284
[SPEAKER_00]: It makes the game better, too.

606
00:38:00,524 --> 00:38:01,544
[SPEAKER_00]: They're on top of players.

607
00:38:01,584 --> 00:38:05,465
[SPEAKER_00]: And if you notice, a lot of these instant replays, they don't make many mistakes.

608
00:38:05,845 --> 00:38:06,905
[SPEAKER_00]: They don't make many mistakes.

609
00:38:08,169 --> 00:38:10,872
[SPEAKER_01]: Back in the real old days, they had just one umpire.

610
00:38:10,892 --> 00:38:14,115
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, he used to be behind the plate, and then he'd walk out behind the pitcher.

611
00:38:14,155 --> 00:38:14,875
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, I remember that.

612
00:38:15,376 --> 00:38:20,301
[SPEAKER_01]: And they'd tell stories about players cutting from first strike to third strike.

613
00:38:20,881 --> 00:38:23,103
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, I don't remember those times very much, no.

614
00:38:23,264 --> 00:38:31,592
[SPEAKER_01]: No wonder the umpires took a beating when they literally threw bottles at him and things like that.

615
00:38:31,752 --> 00:38:32,953
[SPEAKER_01]: You played in Columbus then.

616
00:38:33,380 --> 00:38:35,202
[SPEAKER_01]: The old Neal Park, was it?

617
00:38:35,602 --> 00:38:37,764
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, Neal Park, that's right.

618
00:38:37,804 --> 00:38:39,966
[SPEAKER_00]: In fact, I stayed at the Neal house, too, there, yeah.

619
00:38:40,806 --> 00:38:48,994
[SPEAKER_00]: And then there was another little hotel that I moved later on out called St. Francis, out North High Street, going out toward the university, out that way.

620
00:38:49,254 --> 00:38:50,755
[SPEAKER_00]: Didn't the players stay in the same place?

621
00:38:51,336 --> 00:38:52,316
[SPEAKER_00]: No, not at home, no.

622
00:38:52,757 --> 00:38:54,178
[SPEAKER_00]: We're on our own at home, see.

623
00:38:54,558 --> 00:38:55,879
[SPEAKER_00]: It's at your expense.

624
00:38:55,940 --> 00:38:58,682
[SPEAKER_00]: When they go on the road, then they house them at one hotel, see.

625
00:38:59,743 --> 00:39:05,806
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, I can remember there was a soldier's home out near the ballpark when we played out there.

626
00:39:06,146 --> 00:39:06,787
[SPEAKER_00]: Fort Hayes?

627
00:39:07,727 --> 00:39:09,828
[SPEAKER_00]: That's right, it was right near Fort Hayes.

628
00:39:09,848 --> 00:39:13,350
[SPEAKER_00]: And there was a soldier's home across the street from the ballpark.

629
00:39:14,211 --> 00:39:19,273
[SPEAKER_00]: And of course now, it was a year or two after that they built the new ballpark.

630
00:39:19,954 --> 00:39:21,755
[SPEAKER_00]: When did the Cardinals get there?

631
00:39:21,895 --> 00:39:24,676
[SPEAKER_00]: The Cardinals went in about 1930, I believe.

632
00:39:24,716 --> 00:39:27,077
[SPEAKER_00]: That's when Cincinnati owned Columbus 28-29.

633
00:39:28,675 --> 00:39:31,216
[SPEAKER_00]: And then they got rid of it and the Cardinals came in there after that.

634
00:39:31,616 --> 00:39:35,178
[SPEAKER_00]: See, we were the Columbus veterans at the time.

635
00:39:35,278 --> 00:39:37,299
[SPEAKER_00]: Then they became the Columbus Redbirds.

636
00:39:37,779 --> 00:39:38,459
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, the vets.

637
00:39:38,859 --> 00:39:42,601
[SPEAKER_00]: After the veterans in the home there.

638
00:39:42,641 --> 00:39:44,041
[SPEAKER_01]: How big a park was that?

639
00:39:44,682 --> 00:39:46,582
[SPEAKER_00]: It was a pretty good sized ballpark, yes.

640
00:39:46,642 --> 00:39:48,743
[SPEAKER_00]: I believe there was a brewery back in left field.

641
00:39:49,263 --> 00:39:53,025
[SPEAKER_00]: In fact, it ran about 360 feet, 355, something like that.

642
00:39:53,045 --> 00:39:54,506
[SPEAKER_01]: Who managed that club?

643
00:39:55,086 --> 00:39:56,406
[SPEAKER_00]: Nemo Lebo.

644
00:39:56,606 --> 00:39:57,467
[SPEAKER_00]: Old Nemo Lebo.

645
00:39:58,814 --> 00:40:02,356
[SPEAKER_00]: You remember Joe Carr, the old football man?

646
00:40:02,396 --> 00:40:05,057
[SPEAKER_00]: He was president of the club, he run the club, yes.

647
00:40:05,097 --> 00:40:06,498
[SPEAKER_00]: Did you know Nick Collins?

648
00:40:06,538 --> 00:40:07,939
[SPEAKER_00]: Nick was one of our players, yeah.

649
00:40:08,539 --> 00:40:11,520
[SPEAKER_00]: He later went on to manage the association, yeah.

650
00:40:11,920 --> 00:40:13,061
[SPEAKER_01]: He did his home runs as well.

651
00:40:13,081 --> 00:40:17,563
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, he went up with me in Cincinnati, Silas Johnson.

652
00:40:17,663 --> 00:40:21,105
[SPEAKER_00]: Oh, I can't think of some of the other boys.

653
00:40:21,205 --> 00:40:22,946
[SPEAKER_00]: Oscar Eckert went up, pitcher.

654
00:40:24,672 --> 00:40:31,959
[SPEAKER_00]: Nick never could quite cut it when they were... No, I mean, he used to hit .340, .350 in the minor leagues all year, but then he come up odd.

655
00:40:31,999 --> 00:40:32,319
[SPEAKER_00]: Why?

656
00:40:32,379 --> 00:40:32,799
[SPEAKER_00]: I don't know.

657
00:40:32,819 --> 00:40:34,881
[SPEAKER_00]: Whether he had a nuclear complex or...

658
00:40:34,941 --> 00:40:40,806
[SPEAKER_01]: I think he was up with the Yankees, too, in the mid-twenties, just at the time Earl Combs came up, and they already had Neusman Roof.

659
00:40:40,946 --> 00:40:41,166
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

660
00:40:41,186 --> 00:40:43,028
[SPEAKER_01]: So it was just bad timing.

661
00:40:43,048 --> 00:40:49,934
[SPEAKER_01]: I think that's what we agreed upon when I started with him, that he just, every chance he got seemed to be at a moment when there were

662
00:40:50,301 --> 00:40:51,742
[SPEAKER_01]: pretty tough competition.

663
00:40:52,323 --> 00:40:52,663
[SPEAKER_00]: I don't know.

664
00:40:52,683 --> 00:40:58,867
[SPEAKER_00]: I do remember one incident very well in the Polo Grounds when Nick was playing with us in Cincinnati in the early part of 1930.

665
00:40:59,048 --> 00:41:10,316
[SPEAKER_00]: I happened to be at second base and Travis Jackson, Stoney Jackson, the shortstop, and there was a pause, a minute or two, something had happened.

666
00:41:10,356 --> 00:41:11,477
[SPEAKER_00]: And Cullum was the

667
00:41:12,157 --> 00:41:15,160
[SPEAKER_00]: the hitter, and he said to me, watch how they pitch this fella.

668
00:41:15,200 --> 00:41:19,443
[SPEAKER_00]: Now he's been around here for, he had three or four tries already in the big leagues and never made it.

669
00:41:19,803 --> 00:41:24,067
[SPEAKER_00]: He'll start pitches around his letters and he'll keep going up and he'll keep missing them.

670
00:41:24,327 --> 00:41:26,049
[SPEAKER_00]: And sure enough, that's the way they pitched him.

671
00:41:26,389 --> 00:41:29,932
[SPEAKER_00]: And he wouldn't lay off the ball, he'd swing at the ball and invariably miss it.

672
00:41:31,313 --> 00:41:38,183
[SPEAKER_00]: See, he was more of a ball, he did the ball more of a low ball hitter, about waist high, but they would start by the letters and keep going up.

673
00:41:38,824 --> 00:41:41,709
[SPEAKER_00]: And he'd keep off and let the pitch, and that's exactly what they did.

674
00:41:43,612 --> 00:41:44,512
[SPEAKER_01]: How about Joe Hauser?

675
00:41:44,532 --> 00:41:45,993
[SPEAKER_01]: Did you ever play with him or against him?

676
00:41:46,033 --> 00:41:46,653
[SPEAKER_00]: Joe Hauser?

677
00:41:46,833 --> 00:41:48,054
[SPEAKER_00]: Yes, in the association.

678
00:41:48,114 --> 00:41:51,255
[SPEAKER_00]: He was with Milwaukee, I believe, when I was with Columbus.

679
00:41:51,656 --> 00:41:52,636
[SPEAKER_00]: Great home run hitter.

680
00:41:52,796 --> 00:41:53,637
[SPEAKER_01]: Yes, yes, Joe.

681
00:41:53,657 --> 00:41:57,238
[SPEAKER_01]: I remember very well the fact that then he hit about 55, 56.

682
00:41:57,778 --> 00:42:02,080
[SPEAKER_01]: He has a record of hitting over 60 home runs in two different leagues.

683
00:42:02,180 --> 00:42:07,383
[SPEAKER_01]: He had 63 with Baltimore in 1930 and 69 with Minneapolis in 1938.

684
00:42:07,403 --> 00:42:08,603
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, well, it was up there somewhere.

685
00:42:08,623 --> 00:42:10,284
[SPEAKER_00]: I know it was in an association, yes.

686
00:42:10,884 --> 00:42:12,025
[SPEAKER_00]: They claimed he had a

687
00:42:13,147 --> 00:42:16,009
[SPEAKER_01]: kind of a band box to hit up in.

688
00:42:16,029 --> 00:42:17,310
[SPEAKER_00]: Did you ever see Nicollet Park?

689
00:42:17,410 --> 00:42:18,711
[SPEAKER_00]: That's what it was called at the time.

690
00:42:18,891 --> 00:42:20,352
[SPEAKER_00]: See, I played in that ballpark.

691
00:42:20,432 --> 00:42:22,233
[SPEAKER_00]: It was a really short ballpark, yeah.

692
00:42:22,534 --> 00:42:24,755
[SPEAKER_00]: You could hit line drives and hit the center field wall.

693
00:42:25,816 --> 00:42:36,503
[SPEAKER_00]: And he had this, he was a left-hand hitter, and his right field at that time, I would say, oh, about 260 feet, about 260, but the fence was up about, oh, 25, 30 feet.

694
00:42:36,864 --> 00:42:38,625
[SPEAKER_00]: But he used to loft the ball, and he'd hit

695
00:42:39,457 --> 00:42:40,878
[SPEAKER_00]: over that 30-40 feet.

696
00:42:41,218 --> 00:42:43,699
[SPEAKER_01]: He was a little irritated.

697
00:42:43,739 --> 00:42:45,620
[SPEAKER_00]: He was up with the athletics for a while.

698
00:42:46,020 --> 00:42:48,221
[SPEAKER_01]: He had a real good major league career ahead of him.

699
00:42:48,241 --> 00:42:50,262
[SPEAKER_01]: He twisted each knee.

700
00:42:50,323 --> 00:42:55,645
[SPEAKER_01]: Once he got his left knee and that finished him for a while, then he got back and the other knee went.

701
00:42:55,765 --> 00:42:58,447
[SPEAKER_01]: And very routine plays.

702
00:42:59,823 --> 00:43:03,665
[SPEAKER_01]: I'd really rented his major league for him, but he could still hit.

703
00:43:03,725 --> 00:43:06,086
[SPEAKER_00]: I believe he went to managing after a little while, too.

704
00:43:06,146 --> 00:43:08,027
[SPEAKER_00]: I think he did briefly.

705
00:43:08,127 --> 00:43:10,469
[SPEAKER_01]: I'm not sure he was too happy in that role.

706
00:43:10,749 --> 00:43:17,132
[SPEAKER_00]: But no, I knew Joe very well, and I'd say playing against him, he seemed to be a real, real nice fella, too.

707
00:43:17,292 --> 00:43:18,593
[SPEAKER_01]: I spent a whole day with him.

708
00:43:18,633 --> 00:43:19,814
[SPEAKER_01]: He lives up in Sheboygan.

709
00:43:19,974 --> 00:43:21,955
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, way up in north Wisconsin up there.

710
00:43:21,975 --> 00:43:23,236
[SPEAKER_01]: He was a real great guy.

711
00:43:26,677 --> 00:43:29,600
[SPEAKER_01]: He's a wonderful man.

712
00:43:29,620 --> 00:43:37,446
[SPEAKER_01]: I enjoy talking with him.

713
00:43:37,566 --> 00:43:40,908
[SPEAKER_01]: Did you ever meet Luke Boone?

714
00:43:40,968 --> 00:43:42,710
[SPEAKER_00]: Not Ike, Lute.

715
00:43:43,350 --> 00:43:45,432
[SPEAKER_00]: No, you mean the infielder.

716
00:43:46,152 --> 00:43:47,353
[SPEAKER_00]: He was with us at Columbus.

717
00:43:47,833 --> 00:43:49,315
[SPEAKER_00]: I was wondering if you knew him.

718
00:43:49,355 --> 00:43:51,216
[SPEAKER_00]: Yes, in 1928 he was our shortstop.

719
00:43:51,496 --> 00:43:53,758
[SPEAKER_00]: We got a boy by the name of Fritz Nikolai.

720
00:43:54,616 --> 00:43:55,676
[SPEAKER_00]: came in and replaced him.

721
00:43:55,836 --> 00:43:59,478
[SPEAKER_00]: Emmett McCann was one of our players.

722
00:44:00,598 --> 00:44:00,918
[SPEAKER_00]: D.B.

723
00:44:00,978 --> 00:44:03,259
[SPEAKER_00]: Foss was the third baseman.

724
00:44:04,079 --> 00:44:08,661
[SPEAKER_00]: Oh yeah, and how about the right fielder that was up in the big leagues, came down.

725
00:44:10,041 --> 00:44:10,602
[SPEAKER_00]: Ernie Neese?

726
00:44:10,842 --> 00:44:12,082
[SPEAKER_00]: Bernie Neese.

727
00:44:12,202 --> 00:44:14,143
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, Bernie Neese was in there.

728
00:44:14,163 --> 00:44:15,823
[SPEAKER_01]: Luke Boone was up in Pittsburgh now.

729
00:44:15,843 --> 00:44:16,544
[SPEAKER_01]: He's still in there.

730
00:44:16,584 --> 00:44:17,364
[SPEAKER_01]: He's about 86.

731
00:44:17,584 --> 00:44:18,544
[SPEAKER_00]: Oh, he's got to be a lot...

732
00:44:18,564 --> 00:44:20,265
[SPEAKER_00]: I know he's a lot older than I am, yeah.

733
00:44:20,777 --> 00:44:23,219
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, Danny was a... wasn't it called Danny Boone?

734
00:44:23,239 --> 00:44:23,760
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, that's right.

735
00:44:23,780 --> 00:44:24,420
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, that's right.

736
00:44:24,600 --> 00:44:25,981
[SPEAKER_01]: I remember him very, very well.

737
00:44:26,061 --> 00:44:29,164
[SPEAKER_01]: Played a lot with St. Paul and he played some of Louisville.

738
00:44:29,664 --> 00:44:29,985
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

739
00:44:30,225 --> 00:44:31,185
[SPEAKER_00]: See, he was a lot older than I.

740
00:44:31,206 --> 00:44:36,069
[SPEAKER_00]: When I joined the club, I was just about 19, 20 years old, something like that.

741
00:44:36,089 --> 00:44:38,331
[SPEAKER_00]: Were you with the 28th St. Paul's Club?

742
00:44:39,052 --> 00:44:39,232
[SPEAKER_00]: 28?

743
00:44:39,272 --> 00:44:39,993
[SPEAKER_00]: 28, yes.

744
00:44:40,013 --> 00:44:41,254
[SPEAKER_01]: I've got a big picture of that team.

745
00:44:41,274 --> 00:44:42,575
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, I'm on it, yeah.

746
00:44:43,485 --> 00:44:44,646
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, I didn't realize that.

747
00:44:45,086 --> 00:44:47,447
[SPEAKER_01]: You got it framed and like... Yeah, 28.

748
00:44:47,527 --> 00:44:49,887
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, now, it's according to when they took the picture, now.

749
00:44:50,228 --> 00:44:52,849
[SPEAKER_00]: If they took it late in the season, I was on it, but...

750
00:44:52,909 --> 00:44:56,290
[SPEAKER_00]: In 28, I joined them from Danville, Illinois, in a 3-I.

751
00:44:56,650 --> 00:45:02,592
[SPEAKER_00]: Went over to Indianapolis, where they were playing there, and... What's the name?

752
00:45:03,893 --> 00:45:06,534
[SPEAKER_00]: Nebo Lebo put me right in the second ballgame.

753
00:45:06,954 --> 00:45:08,915
[SPEAKER_00]: I got there while the first game was going on.

754
00:45:09,235 --> 00:45:12,276
[SPEAKER_00]: He said, get in uniform, and I went in and played the second game of the ballgame.

755
00:45:12,963 --> 00:45:16,306
[SPEAKER_00]: stay there two or three more days and move on to Columbus.

756
00:45:16,326 --> 00:45:20,008
[SPEAKER_00]: I played approximately two weeks.

757
00:45:20,129 --> 00:45:21,750
[SPEAKER_01]: You were in Indianapolis though?

758
00:45:21,970 --> 00:45:23,791
[SPEAKER_01]: No, against Indianapolis.

759
00:45:23,811 --> 00:45:24,712
[SPEAKER_01]: Remember Eddie Sicking?

760
00:45:26,273 --> 00:45:26,694
[SPEAKER_01]: Sicking?

761
00:45:26,754 --> 00:45:27,414
[SPEAKER_01]: Second baseman?

762
00:45:27,494 --> 00:45:31,117
[SPEAKER_01]: Oh yeah, yes, yes, yeah, very well, yeah.

763
00:45:32,478 --> 00:45:35,000
[SPEAKER_00]: Freddie Haney was the third baseman that year over there, yeah.

764
00:45:35,440 --> 00:45:37,262
[SPEAKER_00]: Amy Leedy, remember Amy Leedy?

765
00:45:37,682 --> 00:45:38,683
[SPEAKER_00]: Clyde Barnhart.

766
00:45:39,482 --> 00:45:41,264
[SPEAKER_01]: Those are old timers.

767
00:45:41,704 --> 00:45:43,086
[SPEAKER_01]: He was in Hagerstown, Maryland.

768
00:45:43,426 --> 00:45:45,388
[SPEAKER_01]: He was just there last summer.

769
00:45:45,408 --> 00:45:51,253
[SPEAKER_00]: Joe Sprintz, the catcher, and the two brothers lived in Indianapolis.

770
00:45:51,373 --> 00:45:54,256
[SPEAKER_00]: One went up to the Cincinnati as a pitcher and one was a catcher.

771
00:45:54,316 --> 00:46:00,702
[SPEAKER_00]: Riddle, Elmer Riddle, John Riddle, they played there in Indianapolis also.

772
00:46:00,722 --> 00:46:03,065
[SPEAKER_01]: You meet a lot of players once you've been down.

773
00:46:05,625 --> 00:46:14,056
[SPEAKER_00]: I got a picture inside that I'll show you a little later on.

774
00:46:14,096 --> 00:46:17,380
[SPEAKER_00]: In fact, I'd like to show you my little room that I fixed up myself.

775
00:46:42,053 --> 00:46:48,237
[SPEAKER_00]: He was ailing at the time and he used to play a lot of golf at the Westchester Country Club that was very, very hilly.

776
00:46:48,658 --> 00:46:50,019
[SPEAKER_00]: Hilly, rocky and all like that.

777
00:46:50,419 --> 00:46:53,921
[SPEAKER_00]: And the babe got so that he couldn't walk those hills anymore.

778
00:46:53,961 --> 00:46:59,305
[SPEAKER_00]: So he used to come out of Bayside, Long Island, which was about, oh, five minutes from my home.

779
00:46:59,833 --> 00:47:03,215
[SPEAKER_00]: and play out there that it was all flat and he could play.

780
00:47:03,535 --> 00:47:07,857
[SPEAKER_00]: And we used to have a lot of fun playing with him or behind him or in front of him.

781
00:47:08,237 --> 00:47:13,400
[SPEAKER_00]: And then we'd come in and we'd sit down and he would tell us some stories about his...

782
00:47:14,220 --> 00:47:20,283
[SPEAKER_00]: In fact, the one time that I played with him in 1945, I almost led the American League in hitting.

783
00:47:20,744 --> 00:47:23,306
[SPEAKER_00]: I lost the last day of the season.

784
00:47:23,687 --> 00:47:25,088
[SPEAKER_00]: George Stern was beating me out.

785
00:47:25,388 --> 00:47:34,356
[SPEAKER_00]: I'll never forget, Babe, after we got in that day, after the game was over, he said, Hey, I understand that you almost led the American League in hitting.

786
00:47:34,657 --> 00:47:39,741
[SPEAKER_00]: He said the American League must have gone far back and you almost led it.

787
00:47:39,761 --> 00:47:40,902
[SPEAKER_00]: Babe was a real good fellow.

788
00:47:41,843 --> 00:47:44,746
[SPEAKER_00]: In fact, he never used your name at all.

789
00:47:47,075 --> 00:47:48,276
[SPEAKER_00]: You know, see we played against him.

790
00:47:48,296 --> 00:47:50,577
[SPEAKER_00]: He went up to the Boston Braves when I was at Brooklyn.

791
00:47:50,917 --> 00:47:56,780
[SPEAKER_00]: And then a year or two later when we were traded, they released him at Boston and he came down to Brooklyn as a coach.

792
00:47:57,681 --> 00:48:01,183
[SPEAKER_00]: And he used to give on exhibitions and hitting between double-headers and all that.

793
00:48:01,423 --> 00:48:02,944
[SPEAKER_00]: He'd hit for 10 minutes at a time.

794
00:48:03,344 --> 00:48:04,845
[SPEAKER_01]: Do you think he would have made a manager?

795
00:48:04,885 --> 00:48:05,786
[SPEAKER_01]: He always wanted a manager.

796
00:48:05,886 --> 00:48:10,268
[SPEAKER_00]: Yes, but I think the New York Yankees offered him a job at Newark.

797
00:48:10,768 --> 00:48:13,690
[SPEAKER_00]: But I think he wanted to manage the Yankees themselves.

798
00:48:14,339 --> 00:48:17,520
[SPEAKER_00]: Now, it's hard to say if he'd have been a good manager.

799
00:48:17,580 --> 00:48:18,281
[SPEAKER_00]: You can't tell.

800
00:48:18,701 --> 00:48:19,481
[SPEAKER_01]: You'd have to see.

801
00:48:19,721 --> 00:48:21,282
[SPEAKER_01]: Good players don't always make good managers.

802
00:48:21,302 --> 00:48:21,682
[SPEAKER_00]: That's right.

803
00:48:21,702 --> 00:48:22,022
[SPEAKER_00]: It's true.

804
00:48:22,042 --> 00:48:22,382
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

805
00:48:25,183 --> 00:48:32,226
[SPEAKER_01]: So, well, you went in 1939.

806
00:48:32,266 --> 00:48:33,787
[SPEAKER_01]: You played 81 games.

807
00:48:33,847 --> 00:48:35,027
[SPEAKER_01]: Were you injured that year?

808
00:48:35,047 --> 00:48:35,727
[SPEAKER_01]: What, 39?

809
00:48:35,747 --> 00:48:36,768
[SPEAKER_01]: 39.

810
00:48:36,928 --> 00:48:37,628
[SPEAKER_01]: With Boston.

811
00:48:38,704 --> 00:48:40,065
[SPEAKER_00]: Oh no, 1939.

812
00:48:40,386 --> 00:48:46,851
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, I think that was the year that they brought up, Casey brought up Sidney Sispy from Hartford, Connecticut.

813
00:48:47,191 --> 00:48:49,373
[SPEAKER_00]: And he asked me if I would work with the kid.

814
00:48:49,393 --> 00:48:50,334
[SPEAKER_00]: He said, don't you worry.

815
00:48:50,354 --> 00:48:54,197
[SPEAKER_00]: He said that you'll have a job here as a player or as a player coach.

816
00:48:54,578 --> 00:48:55,499
[SPEAKER_00]: You work with this kid.

817
00:48:56,019 --> 00:48:58,741
[SPEAKER_00]: And I believe that was the year that I didn't play too many games.

818
00:48:59,522 --> 00:49:01,444
[SPEAKER_00]: Oh, and that was the year that I hurt my knee also.

819
00:49:01,644 --> 00:49:02,945
[SPEAKER_00]: Yes, that same year.

820
00:49:03,325 --> 00:49:06,088
[SPEAKER_00]: And they brought him up and he filled in while I was hurt.

821
00:49:06,108 --> 00:49:06,488
[SPEAKER_00]: I was out

822
00:49:07,245 --> 00:49:08,246
[SPEAKER_00]: Oh, practically two months.

823
00:49:09,346 --> 00:49:14,209
[SPEAKER_00]: Dick Bartel at the Cubs at that time run into me at second base and wrenched my knee.

824
00:49:14,689 --> 00:49:21,433
[SPEAKER_00]: There was no break of the bones, but he tore my internal lateral ligament, which is why I was told.

825
00:49:21,453 --> 00:49:24,095
[SPEAKER_00]: It took me about two months before I got back in the game.

826
00:49:24,435 --> 00:49:25,496
[SPEAKER_00]: Now I remember very well.

827
00:49:25,896 --> 00:49:26,736
[SPEAKER_01]: That was the incident.

828
00:49:26,756 --> 00:49:28,818
[SPEAKER_01]: Was this a stolen base attempt?

829
00:49:28,858 --> 00:49:30,759
[SPEAKER_00]: No, it was a double play thing.

830
00:49:30,819 --> 00:49:31,739
[SPEAKER_00]: He was running on a plane.

831
00:49:31,759 --> 00:49:36,262
[SPEAKER_00]: He got there in time and hit me like on a football block and hit me right across my knee.

832
00:49:40,078 --> 00:49:41,539
[SPEAKER_00]: No, it was more of a block.

833
00:49:41,559 --> 00:49:47,685
[SPEAKER_00]: He was trying to break up the double play and that's what happened.

834
00:49:48,145 --> 00:49:49,266
[SPEAKER_00]: Then you could do it.

835
00:49:49,306 --> 00:49:50,647
[SPEAKER_00]: You can't do it no more, you know.

836
00:49:51,228 --> 00:49:52,629
[SPEAKER_00]: Now they call both runners out.

837
00:49:52,649 --> 00:49:55,651
[SPEAKER_00]: You go out of your way to get an infielder.

838
00:49:56,192 --> 00:50:02,477
[SPEAKER_00]: If you can slide at an infielder and either touch the base with your hands or your feet, it's alright.

839
00:50:02,517 --> 00:50:03,578
[SPEAKER_00]: You can still knock him down.

840
00:50:04,269 --> 00:50:10,151
[SPEAKER_00]: break up the double play, but if you go out of your way and you cannot reach the bed, they'll call both men out, the hitter and also the runner.

841
00:50:11,051 --> 00:50:14,632
[SPEAKER_01]: Did you get any scraps about people fighting you like that?

842
00:50:14,912 --> 00:50:15,172
[SPEAKER_00]: No.

843
00:50:15,212 --> 00:50:19,794
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, we've had little scraps and all like that, but you go in and try to break it up and all like that.

844
00:50:20,594 --> 00:50:21,514
[SPEAKER_00]: I'm a little too small.

845
00:50:21,554 --> 00:50:25,316
[SPEAKER_00]: I didn't want to try to get in any of those battles with some of those big guys.

846
00:50:27,916 --> 00:50:32,678
[SPEAKER_01]: Then in 40, you went over to the Giants.

847
00:50:33,254 --> 00:50:35,996
[SPEAKER_00]: Yes, that was around June or July.

848
00:50:36,016 --> 00:50:41,439
[SPEAKER_00]: I guess it was the cut-down time or the ending of the trading season.

849
00:50:41,759 --> 00:50:43,780
[SPEAKER_00]: The Boston Club was in bad shape at the time.

850
00:50:43,801 --> 00:50:45,982
[SPEAKER_00]: They traded both Lopez and myself.

851
00:50:46,022 --> 00:50:48,303
[SPEAKER_00]: Lopez to Pittsburgh and I went to New York.

852
00:50:48,924 --> 00:50:51,225
[SPEAKER_00]: And I was more of a part-time player over in New York.

853
00:50:51,545 --> 00:50:57,529
[SPEAKER_00]: They had Billy Jurgis, Lighthead, Mellot even played third base some in that year.

854
00:50:59,318 --> 00:51:00,739
[SPEAKER_00]: Played 88 games with him.

855
00:51:00,999 --> 00:51:02,420
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, I mean, I know I just filled in.

856
00:51:02,440 --> 00:51:04,241
[SPEAKER_00]: I didn't pinch Ted quite a bit, see?

857
00:51:04,261 --> 00:51:05,562
[SPEAKER_01]: How did you like Bill Terry?

858
00:51:05,962 --> 00:51:06,603
[SPEAKER_00]: Bill was a good man.

859
00:51:06,623 --> 00:51:07,604
[SPEAKER_00]: A real good baseball man.

860
00:51:07,624 --> 00:51:08,884
[SPEAKER_00]: A good, strict manager.

861
00:51:09,305 --> 00:51:11,386
[SPEAKER_00]: He was more like the McGraw type of manager.

862
00:51:12,087 --> 00:51:23,655
[SPEAKER_00]: And he was the one that I could have stayed with the Giants the following year, but he asked me if I would like to take the Jersey City club as a manager for him, or come back and be a utility player.

863
00:51:23,715 --> 00:51:25,757
[SPEAKER_00]: So the money was going to be the same thing.

864
00:51:25,777 --> 00:51:27,378
[SPEAKER_00]: So I said, well, let me chance this.

865
00:51:28,027 --> 00:51:29,607
[SPEAKER_00]: This manager's job.

866
00:51:30,067 --> 00:51:31,388
[SPEAKER_00]: I went over there for that one year.

867
00:51:33,868 --> 00:51:35,729
[SPEAKER_01]: Then I went back to the Boston Club.

868
00:51:35,949 --> 00:51:36,769
[SPEAKER_01]: You were a playing manager?

869
00:51:36,809 --> 00:51:45,471
[SPEAKER_00]: Playing manager until they sent me Mickey Wittig, the second baseman, and I took myself out off the list and put him on.

870
00:51:45,531 --> 00:51:46,591
[SPEAKER_00]: I played for about a month.

871
00:51:47,551 --> 00:51:54,313
[SPEAKER_01]: They played in Roosevelt Stadium, the one that was built by

872
00:51:55,381 --> 00:52:00,664
[SPEAKER_00]: during the WPA projects.

873
00:52:00,864 --> 00:52:05,107
[SPEAKER_00]: Opening day, we had 62,000 tickets sold.

874
00:52:05,427 --> 00:52:09,629
[SPEAKER_00]: The property holds about 25,000 people.

875
00:52:09,649 --> 00:52:16,013
[SPEAKER_00]: But that was the time Mayor Hague would go all around the city, and if he handed you five tickets, you better take them and just pay for them.

876
00:52:16,273 --> 00:52:17,494
[SPEAKER_00]: And then he'd let you get rid of them.

877
00:52:17,534 --> 00:52:19,255
[SPEAKER_00]: We had 62,100-some-odd paid admissions.

878
00:52:23,799 --> 00:52:24,279
[SPEAKER_01]: for that day.

879
00:52:24,319 --> 00:52:25,380
[SPEAKER_01]: I remember that very, very well.

880
00:52:25,400 --> 00:52:27,361
[SPEAKER_01]: I've seen pictures of the ballpark that day.

881
00:52:27,461 --> 00:52:28,741
[SPEAKER_00]: They'd only seated about 25,000.

882
00:52:28,781 --> 00:52:32,803
[SPEAKER_01]: Was that an old ballpark?

883
00:52:33,064 --> 00:52:35,645
[SPEAKER_00]: Oh no, at that time it was new.

884
00:52:36,345 --> 00:52:38,786
[SPEAKER_00]: They used to play what they call old west side ballpark.

885
00:52:38,806 --> 00:52:42,608
[SPEAKER_00]: It was a real, I would say like a semi-pro ballpark.

886
00:52:43,008 --> 00:52:49,852
[SPEAKER_00]: Seated maybe 5,000 or 6,000 people and fences were about ready to fall down and then they built the Roosevelt Stadium.

887
00:52:51,677 --> 00:52:54,738
[SPEAKER_01]: quite a rivalry there between Newark and New Jersey.

888
00:52:54,998 --> 00:52:58,220
[SPEAKER_00]: There are two clubs that are owned by the New York ball clubs.

889
00:52:58,540 --> 00:52:59,801
[SPEAKER_00]: One the Giants and one the Yankees.

890
00:53:00,261 --> 00:53:09,005
[SPEAKER_00]: But the Yankees had a tremendous, powerful club over in Newark, which they had in Kansas City also at the time and had one at Binghamton, New York.

891
00:53:11,045 --> 00:53:12,446
[SPEAKER_00]: In Virginia they had another one.

892
00:53:13,166 --> 00:53:14,867
[SPEAKER_00]: They had a bunch of good ball players.

893
00:53:15,487 --> 00:53:21,510
[SPEAKER_00]: In fact, they were one of the first clubs or teams that

894
00:53:21,917 --> 00:53:23,598
[SPEAKER_00]: Had the farm system, started the farm system.

895
00:53:23,859 --> 00:53:29,884
[SPEAKER_00]: Cardinals, the New York Yankees, and then the Brooklyn Dodgers were the ones built when Mr. Rickey went over there.

896
00:53:29,904 --> 00:53:31,145
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

897
00:53:31,165 --> 00:53:34,788
[SPEAKER_01]: The Cardinals would have a team in each AAA league?

898
00:53:34,808 --> 00:53:35,268
[SPEAKER_01]: That's right.

899
00:53:35,328 --> 00:53:37,970
[SPEAKER_01]: One in each AA, or I guess they just had AA.

900
00:53:38,130 --> 00:53:44,976
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, see, now I can remember being with the Cardinals in 1928, and we had five clubs in Danville, Illinois.

901
00:53:45,797 --> 00:53:49,680
[SPEAKER_00]: We had batting cages that would go out, put you in a hit, it would be in a batting cage.

902
00:53:50,428 --> 00:53:56,814
[SPEAKER_00]: and they'd have maybe six of them around the whole field, and then all balls would be hitting like in that straight center field.

903
00:53:57,434 --> 00:54:00,377
[SPEAKER_00]: No one could be hurt because they had nets along the side.

904
00:54:00,917 --> 00:54:05,681
[SPEAKER_00]: The pitchers, the only one that had to worry about, they had no nets in front of the pitcher, and you would hit balls out.

905
00:54:05,901 --> 00:54:07,302
[SPEAKER_00]: Everybody would be hitting balls in the center field.

906
00:54:07,322 --> 00:54:13,308
[SPEAKER_00]: Then it would, after about an hour or so, they'd call halt and go out and pick up these balls again and start over again.

907
00:54:14,088 --> 00:54:17,171
[SPEAKER_00]: And Mr. Rickey, he's the first one to come in with the numbers, you know, on the back.

908
00:54:17,708 --> 00:54:19,569
[SPEAKER_00]: back in the uniforms.

909
00:54:19,769 --> 00:54:22,731
[SPEAKER_00]: He never knew our names, he just used your number.

910
00:54:22,771 --> 00:54:24,852
[SPEAKER_00]: Mine was 155 in Danville.

911
00:54:24,872 --> 00:54:27,673
[SPEAKER_00]: He'd say 155, but he knew who I was.

912
00:54:27,733 --> 00:54:29,894
[SPEAKER_00]: He'd say 155, had my name alongside it.

913
00:54:30,215 --> 00:54:33,316
[SPEAKER_00]: And he'd say, tell a picture you want to see fastballs, fastballs.

914
00:54:33,336 --> 00:54:34,717
[SPEAKER_00]: And finally say curveball, curveball.

915
00:54:34,977 --> 00:54:36,238
[SPEAKER_00]: And he would grade you

916
00:54:37,393 --> 00:54:44,017
[SPEAKER_00]: I think Sam Breeden was the owner, but he might have been a part owner of the club, too.

917
00:54:44,037 --> 00:54:46,159
[SPEAKER_01]: What did you think of Branch Rickey?

918
00:54:46,179 --> 00:54:49,841
[SPEAKER_00]: He was a good baseball man, a real, real good baseball man.

919
00:54:50,021 --> 00:54:54,284
[SPEAKER_00]: I got to meet him maybe just one time, and I didn't know too much about him.

920
00:54:54,864 --> 00:54:57,466
[SPEAKER_00]: Talked to the contractor.

921
00:54:58,006 --> 00:55:00,888
[SPEAKER_00]: He'd sell you a bill of goods, and then you'd go back and think he was right.

922
00:55:02,242 --> 00:55:04,403
[SPEAKER_01]: He didn't make you feel good about taking cut in pay.

923
00:55:04,703 --> 00:55:05,344
[SPEAKER_00]: That's right, no.

924
00:55:05,664 --> 00:55:07,445
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, he had a lot of those fellas at the Cardinals.

925
00:55:07,505 --> 00:55:14,489
[SPEAKER_00]: Even in the early 30s, other clubs were paying them in $10,000, $8,000, $7,000.

926
00:55:14,689 --> 00:55:20,192
[SPEAKER_00]: He had fellas playing over there for $3,000, $2,500, even like the Dean boys when they first come up.

927
00:55:20,632 --> 00:55:22,833
[SPEAKER_00]: They didn't make hardly any money after all.

928
00:55:22,853 --> 00:55:27,596
[SPEAKER_00]: What he'd do, he'd sign you for a three-year contract and graduate you $1,000 a year.

929
00:55:28,016 --> 00:55:31,138
[SPEAKER_00]: And then maybe the second year, you might have an outstanding year where you should have got

930
00:55:31,823 --> 00:55:35,884
[SPEAKER_00]: Maybe a $5,000 raise, but he had you to that contract.

931
00:55:37,465 --> 00:55:38,885
[SPEAKER_00]: But he was a good baseball man.

932
00:55:39,205 --> 00:55:39,945
[SPEAKER_00]: He was the first one.

933
00:55:40,486 --> 00:55:41,866
[SPEAKER_00]: He believed in speed.

934
00:55:42,526 --> 00:55:43,686
[SPEAKER_00]: Speed was the first thing.

935
00:55:44,127 --> 00:55:44,887
[SPEAKER_00]: Hitting last.

936
00:55:45,587 --> 00:55:46,327
[SPEAKER_00]: Hitting was last.

937
00:55:46,387 --> 00:55:46,667
[SPEAKER_00]: Why?

938
00:55:46,787 --> 00:55:47,248
[SPEAKER_00]: I don't know.

939
00:55:49,185 --> 00:55:51,286
[SPEAKER_01]: They got you out of the Cardinal system.

940
00:55:51,326 --> 00:55:54,246
[SPEAKER_01]: It was a break because they had so many players in the farm.

941
00:55:54,266 --> 00:55:57,147
[SPEAKER_01]: You might have had a tough time working your way up to the top.

942
00:55:57,167 --> 00:55:58,748
[SPEAKER_00]: Another fellow was with us, Rip Collins.

943
00:55:58,768 --> 00:56:00,728
[SPEAKER_00]: Remember Rip Collins, one of the Cardinal men, the Cubs?

944
00:56:01,068 --> 00:56:02,649
[SPEAKER_00]: He was with us at hand the last year, too.

945
00:56:03,009 --> 00:56:04,729
[SPEAKER_00]: See, now later on, they got rid of Rip, too.

946
00:56:05,269 --> 00:56:07,310
[SPEAKER_00]: He weeded out everybody that couldn't run.

947
00:56:07,850 --> 00:56:09,791
[SPEAKER_00]: Jimmy Jordan went to Brooklyn when I went.

948
00:56:10,171 --> 00:56:11,711
[SPEAKER_00]: We were in the same organization, rather.

949
00:56:12,191 --> 00:56:13,131
[SPEAKER_00]: He sold him, too.

950
00:56:13,492 --> 00:56:14,112
[SPEAKER_00]: He couldn't run.

951
00:56:14,192 --> 00:56:15,252
[SPEAKER_00]: Couldn't feel a good hitter.

952
00:56:15,867 --> 00:56:19,449
[SPEAKER_00]: He stayed around and played more of a relief man is what he was.

953
00:56:19,849 --> 00:56:23,191
[SPEAKER_00]: Utility man, but a good player, good everyday player.

954
00:56:24,711 --> 00:56:25,912
[SPEAKER_00]: But he got rid of everybody.

955
00:56:25,932 --> 00:56:27,032
[SPEAKER_00]: I just couldn't run.

956
00:56:27,553 --> 00:56:29,594
[SPEAKER_00]: He'd line us up, hold a second baseman, you had to run.

957
00:56:29,974 --> 00:56:33,295
[SPEAKER_00]: And I would probably be the last one running, or finishing rather.

958
00:56:35,156 --> 00:56:37,778
[SPEAKER_01]: Speed is important, but other things are too.

959
00:56:37,798 --> 00:56:42,240
[SPEAKER_00]: He figured speed first, fielding next, throwing and then hitting.

960
00:56:43,019 --> 00:56:59,145
[SPEAKER_00]: I know later on when I run the Tampa Club down here in 1947, Clyde Sooper was now working for Branch Rickey in Brooklyn, and he and another man used to go all over the United States running schools, and that's the way they used to start.

961
00:56:59,545 --> 00:57:06,628
[SPEAKER_00]: They'd have a regular form, say running first, and you'd take all the outfielders together, run them, and put down their speed.

962
00:57:07,649 --> 00:57:08,549
[SPEAKER_00]: Run good, fast,

963
00:57:09,415 --> 00:57:11,896
[SPEAKER_00]: can't run, second baseman, and so on down there.

964
00:57:11,916 --> 00:57:17,198
[SPEAKER_00]: And then they'd weed out the ones that couldn't run, whether they could hit or not, but took the ones that could run.

965
00:57:18,258 --> 00:57:24,900
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, they had some baseball ability, and then they'd have games, like inter-squad games among themselves, and they would pick out the clean.

966
00:57:25,340 --> 00:57:32,163
[SPEAKER_00]: Say three or four fellows here in Tampa, then they might go to St. Pete, pick up two more there, then go up to Georgia somewhere, pick up three more there.

967
00:57:32,623 --> 00:57:37,865
[SPEAKER_00]: At the end of the year, they've maybe got 100 ball players, and maybe out of that 100, five might make it.

968
00:57:38,865 --> 00:57:39,105
[SPEAKER_00]: See?

969
00:57:40,107 --> 00:57:45,310
[SPEAKER_00]: And they had real good ballplayers, in fact, good fellows that could run, really run.

970
00:57:47,172 --> 00:57:54,056
[SPEAKER_01]: In those days, did you fellows think much about the color barrier, or there being no blacks playing?

971
00:57:54,336 --> 00:57:57,558
[SPEAKER_00]: No, in fact, no one would bring it up.

972
00:57:57,818 --> 00:57:58,559
[SPEAKER_00]: It's a funny thing.

973
00:57:59,279 --> 00:58:00,160
[SPEAKER_00]: See, I can remember

974
00:58:00,996 --> 00:58:06,840
[SPEAKER_00]: My son was born in the fall of 1945, and we had a lot of problems with him.

975
00:58:06,860 --> 00:58:12,724
[SPEAKER_00]: So in 1946, I had a job with Casey Stengel out of Oakland, California, as a player manager.

976
00:58:13,364 --> 00:58:14,785
[SPEAKER_00]: I mean, as a player coach, rather.

977
00:58:15,225 --> 00:58:19,648
[SPEAKER_00]: And I couldn't make it because I was living in Long Island, being out in California, 3,000 miles away.

978
00:58:20,008 --> 00:58:22,810
[SPEAKER_00]: My wife was a nervous wreck on account of the boy being ill.

979
00:58:23,431 --> 00:58:27,793
[SPEAKER_00]: So I stayed out of baseball in 1946 and played with the Bushwick Club.

980
00:58:28,254 --> 00:58:29,975
[SPEAKER_00]: Now, the Bushwick Club played all the

981
00:58:30,452 --> 00:58:55,148
