1131
01:07:48,749 --> 01:07:50,150
[SPEAKER_00]: See, I had been in Boston.

1132
01:07:50,763 --> 01:07:54,807
[SPEAKER_00]: And I had the two-point lead, and I was rained out a doubleheader the last day of the season.

1133
01:07:55,247 --> 01:07:56,468
[SPEAKER_00]: And they played one in New York.

1134
01:07:57,189 --> 01:08:01,052
[SPEAKER_00]: I had a two-point lead, and he came up with three hits to beat me out.

1135
01:08:02,754 --> 01:08:03,715
[SPEAKER_01]: They changed one of those?

1136
01:08:03,915 --> 01:08:05,116
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, they changed one of them.

1137
01:08:05,396 --> 01:08:10,681
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, they said he had already been on first base, went to second, went to third base when

1138
01:08:12,582 --> 01:08:16,643
[SPEAKER_00]: The official score said the games in Chicago rained out.

1139
01:08:16,883 --> 01:08:18,544
[SPEAKER_00]: Sternweiss had a chance to leave the league.

1140
01:08:19,164 --> 01:08:20,725
[SPEAKER_00]: He said, change that every two weeks.

1141
01:08:21,285 --> 01:08:28,108
[SPEAKER_00]: But then he still had to get another one the last time, about which he did.

1142
01:08:28,628 --> 01:08:30,709
[SPEAKER_01]: Everything went well that year, though.

1143
01:08:30,729 --> 01:08:33,270
[SPEAKER_01]: Maybe the last month it didn't go so well.

1144
01:08:33,310 --> 01:08:35,671
[SPEAKER_00]: Dykes helped me a great deal.

1145
01:08:36,772 --> 01:08:38,552
[SPEAKER_00]: I just tired out at the end of the year.

1146
01:08:40,133 --> 01:08:46,915
[SPEAKER_00]: At that particular time, you had to be in 100 ballgames or I believe 400 times at bat to qualify.

1147
01:08:46,935 --> 01:08:48,916
[SPEAKER_00]: I had to play the last time.

1148
01:08:48,936 --> 01:08:52,216
[SPEAKER_00]: I don't even believe I was a 410 or 412.

1149
01:08:54,137 --> 01:08:55,417
[SPEAKER_01]: I had to play, I had to play.

1150
01:08:55,937 --> 01:08:56,918
[SPEAKER_00]: But Jimmy was good to me.

1151
01:08:56,938 --> 01:09:00,359
[SPEAKER_00]: If I'd go up and get two hits in a ballgame, he would take me out.

1152
01:09:00,419 --> 01:09:02,619
[SPEAKER_00]: He wouldn't let me finish the whole ballgame.

1153
01:09:02,639 --> 01:09:03,480
[SPEAKER_00]: He'd give the rest of me up.

1154
01:09:03,880 --> 01:09:08,481
[SPEAKER_00]: Maybe a day, say maybe a fellow was going to pitch and a fellow had come back from the service,

1155
01:09:09,212 --> 01:09:11,853
[SPEAKER_00]: He put Joe Orrengo in there, he wouldn't put me in there.

1156
01:09:12,153 --> 01:09:13,374
[SPEAKER_00]: He wanted me to lead the league.

1157
01:09:13,834 --> 01:09:19,417
[SPEAKER_00]: See, Jimmy was a third baseman, and no third baseman up until that time had led the league, the American League.

1158
01:09:20,498 --> 01:09:21,458
[SPEAKER_00]: And I had a chance to do it.

1159
01:09:21,958 --> 01:09:24,160
[SPEAKER_00]: After that, George Kell did it.

1160
01:09:24,220 --> 01:09:24,980
[SPEAKER_00]: He was the first one.

1161
01:09:25,020 --> 01:09:26,221
[SPEAKER_01]: So you were playing third then?

1162
01:09:26,281 --> 01:09:27,281
[SPEAKER_00]: I was playing third base.

1163
01:09:27,301 --> 01:09:29,162
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, I couldn't play second, I couldn't move around.

1164
01:09:30,863 --> 01:09:33,384
[SPEAKER_00]: I was 38 years old, at least 38 years old.

1165
01:09:33,404 --> 01:09:34,965
[SPEAKER_01]: They say third base is the old folks.

1166
01:09:35,486 --> 01:09:37,048
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, I didn't do too bad.

1167
01:09:37,068 --> 01:09:37,849
[SPEAKER_00]: I got in good shape.

1168
01:09:37,909 --> 01:09:39,111
[SPEAKER_00]: At 45, I got in good shape.

1169
01:09:39,411 --> 01:09:42,796
[SPEAKER_00]: We trained at French Lake Springs, Indiana, and I took those baths.

1170
01:09:43,317 --> 01:09:44,098
[SPEAKER_00]: We had baths there.

1171
01:09:44,118 --> 01:09:46,401
[SPEAKER_00]: I took them, and we had a run in snow.

1172
01:09:46,822 --> 01:09:48,244
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, they had a bridle path there.

1173
01:09:48,264 --> 01:09:50,908
[SPEAKER_00]: They pushed the snow aside, and I got in real good shape.

1174
01:09:50,928 --> 01:09:51,268
[SPEAKER_00]: In fact,

1175
01:09:51,829 --> 01:09:55,990
[SPEAKER_00]: The lightest I've ever been in my baseball career is when I started that season.

1176
01:09:56,030 --> 01:09:56,370
[SPEAKER_00]: 168.

1177
01:09:56,490 --> 01:09:58,751
[SPEAKER_00]: I used to play 72, 74.

1178
01:09:59,871 --> 01:10:04,853
[SPEAKER_00]: And right from the first day, got two hits, two hits, four hits, three hits.

1179
01:10:05,393 --> 01:10:09,894
[SPEAKER_00]: And I was 380 or 40 or 50 for a long time during that year.

1180
01:10:10,214 --> 01:10:11,534
[SPEAKER_00]: I remember that.

1181
01:10:11,614 --> 01:10:14,915
[SPEAKER_00]: I say, last month I was leading the league by 335.

1182
01:10:14,955 --> 01:10:20,357
[SPEAKER_00]: The closest one was, it wasn't even Sternweiss, I think it was Wally Moses, was hitting 310 or 312.

1183
01:10:21,057 --> 01:10:21,237
[SPEAKER_00]: See?

1184
01:10:21,834 --> 01:10:29,401
[SPEAKER_01]: And then I just faded out.

1185
01:10:29,601 --> 01:10:30,682
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, it's a funny thing.

1186
01:10:30,742 --> 01:10:33,825
[SPEAKER_00]: Mrs. Comiskey had called me in two weeks before the season was closed.

1187
01:10:34,429 --> 01:10:35,850
[SPEAKER_00]: And she said, the boys are coming back.

1188
01:10:35,870 --> 01:10:38,091
[SPEAKER_00]: Collaway was coming back, and all the boys were coming back.

1189
01:10:38,211 --> 01:10:41,452
[SPEAKER_00]: The Peace Treaty was signed with Japan, all of that.

1190
01:10:42,273 --> 01:10:46,655
[SPEAKER_00]: And she said, I enjoyed what you've done for us.

1191
01:10:46,715 --> 01:10:56,139
[SPEAKER_00]: She gave me a little bonus, a $500 bonus, but gave me my release two weeks before the season was over, ending at the last day of the season.

1192
01:10:57,120 --> 01:10:59,941
[SPEAKER_00]: I had in my pocket my release, and I could have led the league.

1193
01:11:03,455 --> 01:11:08,598
[SPEAKER_00]: But I had quit in 1941 in the big leagues.

1194
01:11:09,618 --> 01:11:13,540
[SPEAKER_01]: Were you planning to play in 1946?

1195
01:11:13,560 --> 01:11:13,620
[SPEAKER_00]: No.

1196
01:11:13,701 --> 01:11:16,622
[SPEAKER_00]: He wanted me out in case he had a player coach.

1197
01:11:17,002 --> 01:11:22,925
[SPEAKER_00]: In other words, maybe fill in here, pinch hit occasionally or something like that, be on the active list in case he wanted me.

1198
01:11:22,946 --> 01:11:25,307
[SPEAKER_01]: I think it was after 1945.

1199
01:11:25,347 --> 01:11:26,748
[SPEAKER_00]: No, I got my release.

1200
01:11:26,828 --> 01:11:28,368
[SPEAKER_01]: I wasn't going to play.

1201
01:11:28,709 --> 01:11:31,130
[SPEAKER_01]: But before you got that, you're having this good year.

1202
01:11:33,088 --> 01:11:35,048
[SPEAKER_00]: I was struggling, no.

1203
01:11:35,088 --> 01:11:37,029
[SPEAKER_00]: I just figured it was going to be over, yeah.

1204
01:11:37,269 --> 01:11:39,771
[SPEAKER_00]: That's when Casey wanted me to go out to be a player, a coach.

1205
01:11:40,091 --> 01:11:47,854
[SPEAKER_00]: He said, pinch hit, play when you can play a little bit of game here and there, which I figured, well, then maybe I could do that until the kid took the sick on me and I didn't do it.

1206
01:11:48,695 --> 01:11:52,696
[SPEAKER_00]: See, I played about 90 games with the Bushwicks, but... Oh, that's a big schedule, huh?

1207
01:11:52,876 --> 01:11:54,537
[SPEAKER_00]: Oh, yeah, 90 ballgames, yeah.

1208
01:11:55,938 --> 01:11:58,899
[SPEAKER_01]: So did you work that year at home?

1209
01:11:59,039 --> 01:12:00,660
[SPEAKER_00]: No, I just stayed at home.

1210
01:12:01,269 --> 01:12:03,030
[SPEAKER_00]: I played 90 games with the Bushwicks.

1211
01:12:03,070 --> 01:12:10,994
[SPEAKER_00]: I had a game over in Waterbury, Connecticut and another game over in Orange, New Jersey.

1212
01:12:11,514 --> 01:12:13,175
[SPEAKER_00]: Semi-pro.

1213
01:12:13,195 --> 01:12:17,557
[SPEAKER_00]: I used to pick up $125, $140 plus my salary that I was getting with the Bushwicks.

1214
01:12:17,577 --> 01:12:19,518
[SPEAKER_00]: I was wondering if they paid you.

1215
01:12:19,598 --> 01:12:20,739
[SPEAKER_00]: Oh yeah, definitely.

1216
01:12:20,759 --> 01:12:24,981
[SPEAKER_00]: That year I must have made $7,500 or $8,000 and stayed at home.

1217
01:12:31,180 --> 01:12:34,562
[SPEAKER_01]: How was your salary your last few years in the big time?

1218
01:12:35,202 --> 01:12:37,263
[SPEAKER_00]: Oh, I can't remember exactly.

1219
01:12:37,283 --> 01:12:41,665
[SPEAKER_00]: I would run around $12,000, $14,000 at that time, which was big.

1220
01:12:42,466 --> 01:12:46,808
[SPEAKER_00]: I made more money as a coach than I did as a player.

1221
01:12:46,848 --> 01:12:47,868
[SPEAKER_01]: So $47,000 then.

1222
01:12:48,689 --> 01:12:49,629
[SPEAKER_01]: What did you do that year?

1223
01:12:49,709 --> 01:12:51,910
[SPEAKER_01]: I played with the Bush with $47,000.

1224
01:12:51,930 --> 01:12:52,510
[SPEAKER_00]: That was the first year.

1225
01:12:52,530 --> 01:12:53,371
[SPEAKER_00]: I thought that was $46,000.

1226
01:13:00,007 --> 01:13:04,690
[SPEAKER_00]: I came right down here to manage Tampa.

1227
01:13:04,850 --> 01:13:06,111
[SPEAKER_01]: What organization is this now?

1228
01:13:06,551 --> 01:13:09,993
[SPEAKER_00]: It was owned by an individual, Tom Spagola.

1229
01:13:10,233 --> 01:13:11,214
[SPEAKER_00]: Privately owned, yes.

1230
01:13:11,714 --> 01:13:13,495
[SPEAKER_01]: How did you come across here?

1231
01:13:13,575 --> 01:13:16,217
[SPEAKER_00]: Al Lopez is the one who called me.

1232
01:13:16,237 --> 01:13:18,138
[SPEAKER_00]: He wanted to know if I wanted to get back to baseball.

1233
01:13:18,178 --> 01:13:22,000
[SPEAKER_00]: I said yes, I'd like to get back because now the kid is on his way, feeling good.

1234
01:13:22,400 --> 01:13:26,183
[SPEAKER_00]: He said, I've got a chance for you to manage this club now if you want it.

1235
01:13:26,583 --> 01:13:27,864
[SPEAKER_00]: I figured, well, let me go take it.

1236
01:13:28,732 --> 01:13:30,513
[SPEAKER_00]: It was in the Florida International League.

1237
01:13:30,633 --> 01:13:32,114
[SPEAKER_00]: Havana was in the league at that time.

1238
01:13:32,934 --> 01:13:34,214
[SPEAKER_00]: And then I figured, well, I'll take it.

1239
01:13:34,234 --> 01:13:35,815
[SPEAKER_00]: And the salary wasn't too bad.

1240
01:13:35,875 --> 01:13:40,477
[SPEAKER_00]: And he gave me a chance that I would get so much.

1241
01:13:40,657 --> 01:13:42,558
[SPEAKER_00]: If we drew so many people, he'd give me some money.

1242
01:13:42,998 --> 01:13:46,780
[SPEAKER_00]: If we sold money, he'd give me 10% of the sale of players, which I did.

1243
01:13:46,800 --> 01:13:48,061
[SPEAKER_00]: I made a little money that way, too.

1244
01:13:48,661 --> 01:13:50,562
[SPEAKER_00]: And I had a good year with the club.

1245
01:13:50,702 --> 01:13:54,163
[SPEAKER_00]: We played 154 games with a 15-player limit.

1246
01:13:54,223 --> 01:13:56,424
[SPEAKER_00]: Can you imagine?

1247
01:13:56,485 --> 01:13:57,485
[SPEAKER_00]: I had five pitches.

1248
01:13:58,216 --> 01:14:02,238
[SPEAKER_00]: and won 104 ballgames and finished second.

1249
01:14:03,258 --> 01:14:04,139
[SPEAKER_00]: Havana beat me out.

1250
01:14:04,159 --> 01:14:07,220
[SPEAKER_01]: How did you like playing on Cuba there?

1251
01:14:07,560 --> 01:14:08,481
[SPEAKER_00]: I didn't play there, of course.

1252
01:14:08,501 --> 01:14:10,261
[SPEAKER_01]: I had to go down there.

1253
01:14:10,301 --> 01:14:13,023
[SPEAKER_00]: It was quite a rivalry, Tampa and Cuba.

1254
01:14:13,463 --> 01:14:19,225
[SPEAKER_00]: When we played here, we'd have 10,000 or 12,000 people in the stadium and only seated probably about five.

1255
01:14:19,605 --> 01:14:20,686
[SPEAKER_00]: They'd put seats on the field.

1256
01:14:20,706 --> 01:14:25,508
[SPEAKER_00]: When we'd go to Havana, we'd draw 25,000 people.

1257
01:14:25,568 --> 01:14:26,308
[SPEAKER_00]: Really good.

1258
01:14:26,549 --> 01:14:27,129
[SPEAKER_00]: All night games?

1259
01:14:27,807 --> 01:14:29,927
[SPEAKER_00]: All Nikes, all Nikes, and Grand Stadium.

1260
01:14:29,947 --> 01:14:32,448
[SPEAKER_00]: They got a big stadium down there.

1261
01:14:32,488 --> 01:14:35,489
[SPEAKER_00]: No, no, we had more than one state stadium.

1262
01:14:35,729 --> 01:14:38,729
[SPEAKER_00]: Fort Lauderdale, Miami Beach was bad.

1263
01:14:38,749 --> 01:14:39,829
[SPEAKER_00]: They were in the league at the time.

1264
01:14:40,210 --> 01:14:45,791
[SPEAKER_00]: St. Pete, Lakeland, West Palm Beach, Miami, Miami Beach, Havana.

1265
01:14:47,231 --> 01:14:47,971
[SPEAKER_00]: It was a good league.

1266
01:14:48,031 --> 01:14:51,032
[SPEAKER_00]: It was Class C league, but it was better than the C league.

1267
01:14:51,132 --> 01:14:52,012
[SPEAKER_00]: A lot better.

1268
01:14:58,363 --> 01:15:02,464
[SPEAKER_01]: It's like the Yankees in 54.

1269
01:15:02,484 --> 01:15:03,544
[SPEAKER_00]: They won 103.

1270
01:15:04,505 --> 01:15:05,525
[SPEAKER_00]: We won 111.

1271
01:15:05,585 --> 01:15:06,745
[SPEAKER_00]: I was with Cleveland then.

1272
01:15:07,425 --> 01:15:08,986
[SPEAKER_00]: I grew up in Cleveland.

1273
01:15:09,206 --> 01:15:11,706
[SPEAKER_00]: I was with them in 54.

1274
01:15:14,847 --> 01:15:16,508
[SPEAKER_00]: We lost four straight to the Giants.

1275
01:15:16,848 --> 01:15:19,768
[SPEAKER_00]: The funny part, we had to wait for a playoff.

1276
01:15:20,349 --> 01:15:20,869
[SPEAKER_00]: Don't you remember?

1277
01:15:20,889 --> 01:15:22,329
[SPEAKER_00]: The Giants were in the playoff that year.

1278
01:15:22,349 --> 01:15:23,409
[SPEAKER_00]: We had one dependent.

1279
01:15:26,503 --> 01:15:29,926
[SPEAKER_00]: Let's see, Greenberg was one of our owners and someone else.

1280
01:15:30,507 --> 01:15:39,615
[SPEAKER_00]: He asked the Washington Senator Club if they would stay over and play us ball games to keep the club in competition and the Washington Club would refuse it.

1281
01:15:39,635 --> 01:15:46,561
[SPEAKER_00]: So we had to play in a squad games to try to keep in shape and then we would enter the series cold as could be.

1282
01:15:50,864 --> 01:15:56,105
[SPEAKER_00]: They beat us with Dusty Rose hitting what they called in New York those Chinese home runs.

1283
01:15:56,445 --> 01:16:01,247
[SPEAKER_00]: The first one he beat Lemon in the 10th inning with a 250-foot home run.

1284
01:16:01,767 --> 01:16:03,527
[SPEAKER_00]: It just fell in the right field.

1285
01:16:03,968 --> 01:16:05,348
[SPEAKER_00]: It was a shame.

1286
01:16:05,388 --> 01:16:08,429
[SPEAKER_00]: Then the early pitch to three or four hitters in the second game, he got beat.

1287
01:16:09,760 --> 01:16:13,523
[SPEAKER_00]: We never, Mike Garcia lost the one next one and then Lemon come back again.

1288
01:16:13,563 --> 01:16:19,207
[SPEAKER_00]: And they were given Mr. Lopez help not starting Bob Feller in the series.

1289
01:16:19,227 --> 01:16:23,751
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, I think Al used his best pitches and he's trying to win and trying to win.

1290
01:16:24,111 --> 01:16:25,372
[SPEAKER_00]: We had Lemon win Garcia.

1291
01:16:25,392 --> 01:16:27,594
[SPEAKER_00]: I believe all three of them won 20 ballgames.

1292
01:16:31,128 --> 01:16:33,069
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, Feller was more of a spot pitcher, yeah.

1293
01:16:33,409 --> 01:16:37,531
[SPEAKER_00]: Outerman had won 15, and he had spotted, see?

1294
01:16:37,931 --> 01:16:40,892
[SPEAKER_00]: And Al would figure his pitching staff accordingly.

1295
01:16:40,932 --> 01:16:59,020
[SPEAKER_00]: We'd go into New York City against Rashie, Lopez, and Reynolds, and those guys, Al would have Lemon, Wynne, and Garcia to go against them, and he'd pitch Feller in town ahead, or Outerman in town ahead, and maybe Narleski, pitch him four or five innings, bring in Marcy four or five innings, to go into New York with a good pitching staff, see?

1296
01:16:59,782 --> 01:17:01,963
[SPEAKER_01]: Was Oscar Malone with the team?

1297
01:17:02,023 --> 01:17:03,823
[SPEAKER_00]: No, Oscar wasn't with us then.

1298
01:17:04,083 --> 01:17:05,144
[SPEAKER_00]: Oscar was with Woodrow.

1299
01:17:06,044 --> 01:17:08,185
[SPEAKER_00]: Woodrow was mayor then.

1300
01:17:08,665 --> 01:17:13,727
[SPEAKER_00]: Oscar went to St. Louis with Lou.

1301
01:17:13,767 --> 01:17:15,007
[SPEAKER_00]: The Browns were in the league then.

1302
01:17:15,027 --> 01:17:17,308
[SPEAKER_01]: What years were you with Lopez and Cleveland?

1303
01:17:17,508 --> 01:17:23,850
[SPEAKER_00]: Fifty-one, two, three, four, five, I believe.

1304
01:17:23,890 --> 01:17:26,771
[SPEAKER_01]: Was that really a solid team?

1305
01:17:26,791 --> 01:17:27,771
[SPEAKER_01]: They had a great record.

1306
01:17:27,811 --> 01:17:28,592
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, good record.

1307
01:17:30,048 --> 01:17:33,929
[SPEAKER_00]: Great pitching, good hitting, lousy defense.

1308
01:17:34,289 --> 01:17:35,470
[SPEAKER_00]: But we scored enough runs.

1309
01:17:35,490 --> 01:17:36,450
[SPEAKER_00]: We scored a lot of runs.

1310
01:17:37,411 --> 01:17:42,972
[SPEAKER_00]: See, the pitching kept the other clubs from scoring, and we had enough power to get a lot of runs for them to win.

1311
01:17:43,893 --> 01:17:44,913
[SPEAKER_00]: Our defense was terrible.

1312
01:17:45,633 --> 01:17:47,894
[SPEAKER_00]: We had a poor fielding club and a poor running club.

1313
01:17:49,014 --> 01:17:54,196
[SPEAKER_00]: But we just had good power, real good power on the club, and good pitching, terrific pitching.

1314
01:17:54,636 --> 01:17:56,337
[SPEAKER_00]: We had a member, Marcy Nolesky,

1315
01:17:57,288 --> 01:17:59,729
[SPEAKER_00]: 8th and 9th innings, bring those kids in, it was all over.

1316
01:18:00,210 --> 01:18:02,611
[SPEAKER_01]: But Rustin never did much after that, did he?

1317
01:18:02,671 --> 01:18:03,731
[SPEAKER_01]: Boston had some good years.

1318
01:18:03,751 --> 01:18:11,755
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, well, no, Rustin went through until he went to AL, and he said to AL, he wanted to become a starter and hurt his arm.

1319
01:18:13,196 --> 01:18:16,457
[SPEAKER_00]: See, now he started throwing hard for more than three innings.

1320
01:18:16,638 --> 01:18:21,600
[SPEAKER_00]: All he'd go would be about three innings was the limit, and he'd strike anybody out of three innings.

1321
01:18:22,603 --> 01:18:26,926
[SPEAKER_00]: I can remember one time in the Yankee Stadium with Mantle and Pat, and the base was loaded.

1322
01:18:26,946 --> 01:18:31,928
[SPEAKER_00]: We were one run to the good, the ninth inning, and three straight fastballs had struck him out.

1323
01:18:33,289 --> 01:18:34,810
[SPEAKER_00]: Now, base hit, we're done.

1324
01:18:35,290 --> 01:18:39,253
[SPEAKER_00]: And Al, he spoke to Al, and Al said, look, here's what I want you to do.

1325
01:18:39,273 --> 01:18:42,554
[SPEAKER_00]: I said, I want you to ram back and throw strikes and throw as hard as you can.

1326
01:18:43,055 --> 01:18:46,637
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's what the kid did, threw strikes, three of them right in a row, struck Mickey out.

1327
01:18:47,657 --> 01:18:47,918
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

1328
01:18:48,438 --> 01:18:51,980
[SPEAKER_01]: What do you think about this?

1329
01:18:52,774 --> 01:19:00,137
[SPEAKER_01]: a new pitching strategy of having starters, middle, long relievers, and short relievers.

1330
01:19:00,237 --> 01:19:00,638
[SPEAKER_00]: I don't know.

1331
01:19:00,738 --> 01:19:03,039
[SPEAKER_01]: You get a complete game, it's really an unusual thing.

1332
01:19:03,059 --> 01:19:03,919
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, I know.

1333
01:19:03,939 --> 01:19:06,240
[SPEAKER_00]: I see it now quite a bit.

1334
01:19:06,280 --> 01:19:11,723
[SPEAKER_00]: I'm out of the major leagues since 1969, but I know they're doing that a lot in the big leagues now.

1335
01:19:11,743 --> 01:19:12,763
[SPEAKER_00]: They got a long starter.

1336
01:19:12,923 --> 01:19:21,027
[SPEAKER_00]: As soon as the first starter gets out, they got a long relief man comes in, then another man for me, then the cleanup man at the back.

1337
01:19:21,453 --> 01:19:25,336
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, years ago, I can remember, there was no relief pitchers.

1338
01:19:26,016 --> 01:19:31,139
[SPEAKER_00]: And the only time you'd bring a pitcher in is about the eighth inning or ninth inning.

1339
01:19:31,159 --> 01:19:34,662
[SPEAKER_00]: You'd pinch hit for the pitcher and bring in another fellow to finish the ballgame.

1340
01:19:35,242 --> 01:19:37,543
[SPEAKER_00]: But they weren't even called relief pitchers.

1341
01:19:37,563 --> 01:19:39,425
[SPEAKER_00]: They just were another pitcher on your ballclub.

1342
01:19:40,185 --> 01:19:41,406
[SPEAKER_00]: And they'd come in and finish the game.

1343
01:19:41,986 --> 01:19:43,788
[SPEAKER_00]: Then it got to be later on.

1344
01:19:43,908 --> 01:19:47,530
[SPEAKER_00]: In fact, I think Mr. Casey Stengel was the first one who started that.

1345
01:19:48,035 --> 01:19:50,978
[SPEAKER_00]: And he started the Pukun system, too.

1346
01:19:51,118 --> 01:19:54,242
[SPEAKER_00]: Right against left, left against right, and all of that.

1347
01:19:54,262 --> 01:19:56,384
[SPEAKER_00]: What do you think of that?

1348
01:19:56,464 --> 01:19:57,765
[SPEAKER_00]: I think it hurt a lot of ballplayers.

1349
01:19:58,326 --> 01:20:01,009
[SPEAKER_00]: Especially a lot of left-hand hitters that never hit left-handed pitches.

1350
01:20:01,749 --> 01:20:05,553
[SPEAKER_00]: Because they never faced that many.

1351
01:20:06,855 --> 01:20:08,817
[SPEAKER_00]: A good hitter would hit right or left-handed regardless.

1352
01:20:09,311 --> 01:20:13,994
[SPEAKER_01]: I suppose there is an advantage in that curveball coming in from their own side.

1353
01:20:14,014 --> 01:20:18,536
[SPEAKER_00]: If you see them every day, how about the right-handers that face the right-hand pitchers every day?

1354
01:20:18,876 --> 01:20:20,197
[SPEAKER_00]: They got to hit them and they did, yeah.

1355
01:20:20,857 --> 01:20:29,622
[SPEAKER_00]: I remember guys like Bill Terry, Babe Herman, Lefty O'Doul, Mel Ott, they hit against everybody, right and left-handed, and they hit.

1356
01:20:30,583 --> 01:20:34,104
[SPEAKER_00]: And there was a lot of guys before them like Lou Gehrig and Babe Ruth.

1357
01:20:34,124 --> 01:20:35,745
[SPEAKER_01]: Ty Cobb didn't care how they threw.

1358
01:20:35,785 --> 01:20:36,406
[SPEAKER_00]: He hit 400.

1359
01:20:37,357 --> 01:20:39,184
[SPEAKER_00]: Sure, see, I mean, it didn't matter much.

1360
01:20:39,933 --> 01:20:43,336
[SPEAKER_00]: Because at that time, they didn't think about alternating back and forth.

1361
01:20:43,716 --> 01:20:47,779
[SPEAKER_00]: And if I say pinch hit, there'd be maybe one pinch hit in a game for the pitcher.

1362
01:20:48,159 --> 01:20:51,422
[SPEAKER_00]: It'd be the 8th or 9th, and that's the way it'd go.

1363
01:20:51,442 --> 01:20:52,123
[SPEAKER_01]: Pitches could hit.

1364
01:20:52,623 --> 01:20:52,903
[SPEAKER_00]: Oh, yeah.

1365
01:20:52,923 --> 01:20:56,066
[SPEAKER_00]: There were a lot of pitchers in baseball that had good records, yeah.

1366
01:20:56,106 --> 01:20:58,508
[SPEAKER_01]: George Hewley, I think, is more proud of his hitting than his pitching.

1367
01:20:58,528 --> 01:20:59,789
[SPEAKER_00]: He was good, yeah.

1368
01:20:59,809 --> 01:21:00,850
[SPEAKER_00]: I remember George very well.

1369
01:21:01,290 --> 01:21:03,351
[SPEAKER_00]: Red Lucas we had in Cincinnati.

1370
01:21:03,371 --> 01:21:08,275
[SPEAKER_00]: I remember him, and there was a boy by the name of Brame in Pittsburgh.

1371
01:21:08,536 --> 01:21:09,016
[SPEAKER_00]: Good hitter.

1372
01:21:09,568 --> 01:21:12,371
[SPEAKER_00]: They used to use him as a pinch hitter.

1373
01:21:12,551 --> 01:21:13,692
[SPEAKER_00]: We had a couple of guys.

1374
01:21:14,012 --> 01:21:15,814
[SPEAKER_00]: Lemon was a good hitter.

1375
01:21:16,094 --> 01:21:18,317
[SPEAKER_00]: Of course, Bob started as an infield outfielder.

1376
01:21:19,017 --> 01:21:19,378
[SPEAKER_00]: Sure.

1377
01:21:19,438 --> 01:21:23,682
[SPEAKER_01]: Have any Dave Herman recollections?

1378
01:21:24,683 --> 01:21:26,264
[SPEAKER_00]: No, see, I didn't play with Dave.

1379
01:21:26,304 --> 01:21:27,726
[SPEAKER_00]: I played against him.

1380
01:21:28,026 --> 01:21:29,367
[SPEAKER_00]: We were traded for one another.

1381
01:21:31,329 --> 01:21:33,832
[SPEAKER_00]: They'd say a lot of stories, but they say a lot of those things that

1382
01:21:34,475 --> 01:21:37,036
[SPEAKER_00]: You read in the paper that they did not happen.

1383
01:21:37,656 --> 01:21:42,497
[SPEAKER_00]: Like he being hit on the head with a fly ball, I never did see it.

1384
01:21:42,517 --> 01:21:45,318
[SPEAKER_00]: They kid a lot about it, but they say it never did happen.

1385
01:21:45,738 --> 01:21:51,140
[SPEAKER_00]: He did, they said, one time lost the ball in the sun, got in on his shoulder, but it was due to the sun that he lost the ball.

1386
01:21:51,620 --> 01:21:55,241
[SPEAKER_00]: At that time, very few guys wore glasses, sunglasses.

1387
01:21:56,141 --> 01:21:58,942
[SPEAKER_01]: Where did they play their first night ball?

1388
01:22:00,462 --> 01:22:01,263
[SPEAKER_00]: In Cincinnati.

1389
01:22:02,143 --> 01:22:02,483
[SPEAKER_00]: Remember?

1390
01:22:04,138 --> 01:22:10,662
[SPEAKER_00]: I was with Brooklyn.

1391
01:22:10,682 --> 01:22:12,323
[SPEAKER_00]: Philly played the first game.

1392
01:22:12,664 --> 01:22:15,466
[SPEAKER_00]: At that time, each club played only one game.

1393
01:22:16,807 --> 01:22:20,309
[SPEAKER_01]: We came in later on.

1394
01:22:21,190 --> 01:22:25,753
[SPEAKER_00]: They had pretty good lights at the time compared to some of the minor league lights that I see.

1395
01:22:28,495 --> 01:22:30,877
[SPEAKER_00]: I was afraid to go up to the plate, I'll be honest with you.

1396
01:22:31,584 --> 01:22:35,127
[SPEAKER_00]: that they were real, and how these kids played with some of these lights, I do not know.

1397
01:22:35,627 --> 01:22:36,548
[SPEAKER_01]: I feel sorry for them.

1398
01:22:36,568 --> 01:22:39,190
[SPEAKER_01]: You didn't have lights though while you were still in the minor league?

1399
01:22:39,250 --> 01:22:42,813
[SPEAKER_01]: I think they came in about the next year or so, after you came up to Cincinnati.

1400
01:22:43,913 --> 01:22:58,845
[SPEAKER_00]: No, well they didn't have, they had, once in a while, we played an exhibition game in Indianapolis, had these portable lights, and Bob Musial was on our club, Bob used to hit infield for us a little bit, and he used to hit balls up in the air, over the lights, and you couldn't see the ball.

1401
01:22:59,859 --> 01:23:03,720
[SPEAKER_00]: You'd wait until they'd come down where it got into the lights, and then it was too late to catch it.

1402
01:23:04,360 --> 01:23:07,761
[SPEAKER_00]: But there was portable lights generated on the side.

1403
01:23:11,042 --> 01:23:14,924
[SPEAKER_01]: I guess the first minor league lights were pretty dim.

1404
01:23:15,684 --> 01:23:16,784
[SPEAKER_00]: They had to be, yeah.

1405
01:23:17,544 --> 01:23:18,385
[SPEAKER_00]: They had to be.

1406
01:23:19,005 --> 01:23:22,406
[SPEAKER_00]: How'd you travel around in the minor leagues?

1407
01:23:22,726 --> 01:23:25,547
[SPEAKER_00]: In Danville in the 3-I was buses.

1408
01:23:26,227 --> 01:23:28,868
[SPEAKER_00]: At the New England League we had three cars.

1409
01:23:29,762 --> 01:23:43,506
[SPEAKER_00]: One was a big locomotive, it would carry about seven or eight boys, and the other two cars would bring the club and the manager with us, and maybe a man to take care of the reservations and all like that, hotels and things of that sort.

1410
01:23:44,606 --> 01:23:47,567
[SPEAKER_00]: We'd have maybe two road trips going up to Maine.

1411
01:23:47,627 --> 01:23:54,809
[SPEAKER_00]: We'd stay up there for two or three days, play Portland, Lewis, Auburn, and then down to Manchester, New Hampshire, things like that.

1412
01:23:54,829 --> 01:23:57,910
[SPEAKER_00]: That would be the only time probably we'd stay overnight, see.

1413
01:23:58,110 --> 01:23:59,310
[SPEAKER_01]: You'd fly around by jet?

1414
01:24:00,750 --> 01:24:03,711
[SPEAKER_00]: And then, of course, when I got to the Association, we traveled by train, see?

1415
01:24:04,592 --> 01:24:07,373
[SPEAKER_00]: Which to me, that was a great thing.

1416
01:24:07,393 --> 01:24:11,314
[SPEAKER_00]: They had big leagues, trained in big leagues, too, until late.

1417
01:24:11,814 --> 01:24:13,455
[SPEAKER_00]: I can't remember what year it was.

1418
01:24:13,735 --> 01:24:15,195
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, I wasn't a player when we were flying.

1419
01:24:15,235 --> 01:24:16,576
[SPEAKER_00]: I became a coach.

1420
01:24:17,196 --> 01:24:18,556
[SPEAKER_00]: I was coaching when we started flying.

1421
01:24:18,676 --> 01:24:20,937
[SPEAKER_01]: How about those long train rides from Boston to St. Louis?

1422
01:24:20,997 --> 01:24:22,058
[SPEAKER_00]: Oh, we've had that, yeah.

1423
01:24:22,358 --> 01:24:23,438
[SPEAKER_00]: Twenty-four hours it took us.

1424
01:24:23,458 --> 01:24:23,738
[SPEAKER_00]: Is that right?

1425
01:24:23,978 --> 01:24:24,358
[SPEAKER_00]: Yes, sir.

1426
01:24:24,578 --> 01:24:25,819
[SPEAKER_00]: What did you do all that time?

1427
01:24:25,979 --> 01:24:28,920
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, you'd go to bed and you'd lay down and read.

1428
01:24:29,493 --> 01:24:30,394
[SPEAKER_00]: play cards.

1429
01:24:30,414 --> 01:24:42,203
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, during the war years, one of those 43 or 44, we took a train ride from St. Louis by coach to Pittsburgh.

1430
01:24:42,923 --> 01:24:44,505
[SPEAKER_00]: No, by Pullman of Pittsburgh.

1431
01:24:44,645 --> 01:24:47,347
[SPEAKER_00]: And then get out of the Pullman, got in coaches, all the way into Boston.

1432
01:24:48,328 --> 01:24:53,291
[SPEAKER_00]: They needed the Pullman again to go someplace else and probably carry troops is what they were doing at the time.

1433
01:24:55,757 --> 01:24:56,557
[SPEAKER_00]: It was tough.

1434
01:24:56,597 --> 01:24:57,817
[SPEAKER_00]: Traveling was tough at that time.

1435
01:24:58,537 --> 01:25:00,338
[SPEAKER_00]: Even in the three-eye league, we traveled by bus.

1436
01:25:00,378 --> 01:25:04,759
[SPEAKER_00]: We had to go from Danville, Illinois, over to Quincy, Illinois, right across the whole state.

1437
01:25:05,819 --> 01:25:07,839
[SPEAKER_00]: That's over near Iowa, in that area.

1438
01:25:08,059 --> 01:25:11,380
[SPEAKER_01]: Did you have a regular driver?

1439
01:25:11,540 --> 01:25:12,580
[SPEAKER_00]: Yes.

1440
01:25:12,600 --> 01:25:15,740
[SPEAKER_00]: The bus was owned by the club, but they had a regular driver.

1441
01:25:16,261 --> 01:25:20,961
[SPEAKER_01]: Some of the players in the minor league, you used to tell them how they had to drive cars and buses.

1442
01:25:20,981 --> 01:25:24,882
[SPEAKER_00]: Here, right here in this league, I had Chet Covington, a pitcher.

1443
01:25:25,599 --> 01:25:31,521
[SPEAKER_00]: They used to give them an extra dollar a day meal money for the bus, to drive the bus.

1444
01:25:31,541 --> 01:25:36,802
[SPEAKER_00]: We'd go to Miami or Miami Beach, and we'd go to Hibernia, but we'd leave the bus there.

1445
01:25:36,842 --> 01:25:43,664
[SPEAKER_00]: We'd go over and fly back and pick up the bus, and we'd make a move to Fort Lauderdale, West Palm Beach, and they had it all on there.

1446
01:25:46,222 --> 01:25:49,263
[SPEAKER_01]: What did you do in the off season during all your years?

1447
01:25:49,323 --> 01:25:49,763
[SPEAKER_00]: Not much.

1448
01:25:49,883 --> 01:25:53,625
[SPEAKER_00]: Outside of the war years that I went into a defense plant, I had three years.

1449
01:25:54,185 --> 01:25:57,506
[SPEAKER_00]: I was an iron worker at the time making these gun shields.

1450
01:25:58,346 --> 01:25:59,226
[SPEAKER_00]: I didn't know much about it.

1451
01:25:59,407 --> 01:26:00,387
[SPEAKER_00]: It was easy for me.

1452
01:26:00,407 --> 01:26:09,810
[SPEAKER_00]: They had a whole rivets and nuts that my boss would make up these bolts and all like that, and that was my job.

1453
01:26:10,790 --> 01:26:13,091
[SPEAKER_00]: But other than that, I didn't do any work at all.

1454
01:26:13,111 --> 01:26:14,851
[SPEAKER_01]: Did you do a lot of hunting?

1455
01:26:14,871 --> 01:26:15,251
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, yeah.

1456
01:26:15,271 --> 01:26:18,872
[SPEAKER_00]: I used to do a little bit when I first got married, and then I got away from it.

1457
01:26:18,892 --> 01:26:19,912
[SPEAKER_00]: I used to go up to Connecticut away.

1458
01:26:19,972 --> 01:26:22,993
[SPEAKER_00]: I had friends up there, a lot of birds and all of that, mostly birds.

1459
01:26:23,133 --> 01:26:24,493
[SPEAKER_01]: Was your home still in Long Island?

1460
01:26:24,713 --> 01:26:25,374
[SPEAKER_00]: At that time, yeah.

1461
01:26:25,394 --> 01:26:27,734
[SPEAKER_00]: I'm down here now about 20 years.

1462
01:26:27,754 --> 01:26:29,975
[SPEAKER_01]: During your baseball period, most of you stayed there?

1463
01:26:30,135 --> 01:26:32,235
[SPEAKER_00]: All along, yes.

1464
01:26:33,115 --> 01:26:35,716
[SPEAKER_01]: During the war, I went to Queens College in Greece.

1465
01:26:35,736 --> 01:26:37,056
[SPEAKER_00]: I lived just about a block away.

1466
01:26:37,096 --> 01:26:38,157
[SPEAKER_00]: I lived in Flushing, yeah.

1467
01:26:39,997 --> 01:26:42,498
[SPEAKER_00]: One of the professors bought my house.

1468
01:26:43,639 --> 01:26:46,761
[SPEAKER_00]: I lived in the back end of Flushing where the Old World's Fair used to be.

1469
01:26:48,662 --> 01:26:51,623
[SPEAKER_00]: So my daughter, my daughter didn't apply.

1470
01:26:51,643 --> 01:26:57,366
[SPEAKER_00]: She took the exam at Queens College and she had something like 84, 85 was passed.

1471
01:26:57,626 --> 01:27:03,450
[SPEAKER_00]: Of course, it was a city-owned college and if somebody took it, they wouldn't accept it.

1472
01:27:14,983 --> 01:27:15,363
[SPEAKER_01]: catch it.

1473
01:27:15,403 --> 01:27:17,766
[SPEAKER_01]: He handled the game pretty well.

